// The boss journal: Morrow-Oak, the Starved Grove, the oldest tree in the realm, in each hero's own words (docs/plans/journal-voices.md)
// Morrow-Oak has no spoken lines in the journal; the heroes speak for him. Move keys are cast names (the journal fires on the cast bar's name).
// The idea every page carries: what the Hunger eats stays gone, so the party chooses where the holes go (out at the edge, never the middle), and a hole is a fall, not a death.
// The Grove Remade covers his second form and the first look at the four limbs; the limbs' own moves have their own pages. Deeper Roots is the Hard name of his unfolding.
// Hunger's Reach is the tank's own beat between two Root Lashes (the lane of roots from the nearest hole; Hard layers a second hole's lane).
// Twin Seed, Hungering Dark, Deeper Roots, Wyrm Wing, Frost Fist, Archmage Rune, Hydra Head and Last Gasp are the Hard-only pages.
// The revamp: Sap Tide now overflows every hole as a ring round its own rim (once the grove is remade); The Hunger Stirs and Starved are the light blows that open the second
// and last stretch of the fight. Greenseed (a living seed that closes a hole), Take Root (rings that hold you through the first snap of the petals) and Grafting (a broken limb's
// move passes to its neighbour) are the Extreme-only pages (extreme: true).
(window.JOURNAL_DATA = window.JOURNAL_DATA || {}).morrowoak = {
  // the first time this hero meets him (after the first pull)
  meet: {
    bastion: "An elder tree as tall as a tower, his heartwood split open on a dark with no stars in it. What that dark eats stays eaten. A seed rides one of us, and where it bursts the ground is gone for good, so it is walked to the edge. A hole is a fall. Held.",
    gloomsworn: "A tree tall as a tower, split down the heart on a dark with no stars. What it swallows stays gone. A seed rides one of us, and where it bursts the floor is lost, so it is carried to the edge. A fall is a heavy blow, not a death. I would know.",
    thresher: "Such a tall old fellow, dear, and a great split in his heart where the stars forgot to go. A seed rides one of us, and wherever it bursts the floor is gone, so we carry it to the edge. A fall is only a fall, and tonight nobody has an appointment with one.",
    bloodreaver: "The big one is a tree the size of a tower with a black wound for a heart! Whatever it eats stays eaten. A seed rides one of us, and where it bursts the floor is gone, so we carry it to the edge. A fall hurts, but it doesn't kill. Almost tempting.",
    runeguard: "A tree as tall as a tower, his heartwood split on a dark without stars, and a seed that rides one of us. Carry it to the edge, for wherever it bursts the floor is gone for good. A hole is a fall, not a death.",
    gorgemaw: "My host is a tree like a tower, with a split heart that eats and never says grace. Whatever it takes stays taken. A seed rides one of my friends and bursts into a hole, so we carry it to the rim, where the loss is least. A fall is a bruise. Promising.",
    oathbroker: "The counterparty is an elder tree, tall as a tower, his heartwood split upon a dark without stars. What it eats is not returned. A seed rides one of my co-signatories, and where it bursts the floor is forfeit, so the bearer carries it to the edge. A fall is a penalty, not a ruin.",
    ironpalm: "He is a tree as tall as a tower, split down the heart on a dark with no stars. What the dark eats stays eaten. A seed rides one of us. We carry it to the edge, and let it open there. A fall is a heavy word, not the last one.",
    ranger: "Tree, tall as a tower, heart split open on a dark with no stars in it. Seed rides one of us, and where it bursts, the floor's gone for good. Carry it to the edge. A hole is a fall, not a grave. Easy, if you place it.",
    shadowblade: "The mark is a tower of a tree with a black split for a heart, and the dark in it eats. It plants a seed on one of us, and where it bursts the floor is gone. Carry it to the edge. I kept to the side it cannot watch.",
    pyromancer: "A tree as tall as a tower! I was pleased, until I saw its heart: a split full of starless dark that eats whatever falls in. A seed rides one of us, and where it bursts the floor is gone forever, so we carry it to the edge. A fall hurts.",
    thermist: "Cold read: a tree tall as a tower, a split heart, a dark with no stars that eats. A seed rides one of us, and where it bursts the floor is gone for good. Carry it to the edge. Hot read: I wanted to run. A fall is only a fall. I cooled, and stayed.",
    driftblade: "He's a tree tall as a tower with a black split for a heart, and a seed lands on one of us, and wherever it bursts the floor is gone for good, so we carry it out through everyone to the edge and let it open there, and falling is only a fall.",
    alembist: "Specimen: a tree tall as a tower, heart split open on a dark with no stars. Observed: whatever it eats stays eaten. A seed rides one of us, and where it bursts the floor is gone for good. Note: carry it to the edge. A fall is a heavy blow, not a death.",
    threadweaver: "A tower of a tree with a split heart full of starless dark that eats and never gives back. A seed rides one of my stitches, and where it bursts the floor is gone, so we pull it out to the edge, away from the rest. A fall is a snag. Watch your footing.",
    marionettist: "The lead is a tree like a tower, with a black split for a heart, and the dark in it eats the stage itself. A seed rides one of the company, and where it bursts the boards are gone for good. Carry it to the wings. (A fall is a bruise, not a curtain.)",
    cleric: "Poor old tree, tall as a tower, his heart split open on a dark with no stars, and it eats. A seed rides one of my dear ones, and where it bursts the floor is gone for good, so we carry it to the edge. A fall is a heavy blow.",
    grovekeeper: "He was an oak once, my kin, tall as a tower, and now a split in his heart full of starless dark that eats. A seed rides one of my lot, and where it bursts the floor is gone. Carry it to the edge. A fall is a bruise, and a bruise heals.",
    chronomancer: "A tree tall as a tower, heart split on a dark with no stars, and whatever it eats stays eaten, even for me. A seed rides one of us, and where it bursts the floor is gone for good. Carry it to the edge; I will have said so. A fall is not an undoing.",
    tidecaller: "A tree as tall as a tower, split down the heart on a dark that eats like an undertow and gives nothing back. A seed rides one of us, and where it bursts the floor is gone, so we carried it to the edge. A fall is a rip, not a drowning.",
    lanternkeeper: "A tree as tall as a tower, a split in his heart where no light has ever been. Whatever it eats stays eaten. A seed rides one of us, and where it bursts the floor is gone, so I walked with them to the edge. A fall is a heavy blow.",
    kenseier: "A tree tall as a tower, a dark with no stars where the heart should be, and whatever it eats stays eaten. A seed rides one of us, and where it bursts the floor is gone for good. Carry it to the edge. A fall is a heavy stroke, not a death.",
    houndmaster: "Old bull of a tree, tall as a tower, black split in his heart that eats and doesn't give back. Seed rides one of us. Where it bursts the floor's gone for good, so walk it out to the edge. A fall's a bruise, not the end. Dogs well back from the split. Sit.",
  },
  // the first time this hero beats him
  win: {
    bastion: "The old tree is down, and the grove is quiet. The room was ours in the end, the holes at the edge and nobody lost in the middle. We kept the ground that mattered. The report is short and good.",
    gloomsworn: "The tree is down and the dark in his heart has closed. The holes are still there. What it eats stays gone, even now. The middle was whole and the party stood on it. I know about starved things.",
    thresher: "He's down, dear, and the dark in his heart has shut like a door. A great many holes at the edge and none in the middle, which is tidy. The grove may sleep now, and so may he.",
    bloodreaver: "The big one's down, and the black wound in his heart has shut! I have a fall's worth of scars and none I'd trade. The holes are still there, all round the rim, like a crown. I'd wear it.",
    runeguard: "The Starved Grove is felled, and I have entered every seed in the margin with the place it burst. The holes lie round the edge like a ring of inscriptions, and the middle stands unmarked. A tidy page.",
    gorgemaw: "My host is felled, and the Hunger closed its mouth at last. A lean larder and a mean feast, and he ate his own grove. I was never so well fed by so hungry a cook. Hollow rim, whole middle.",
    oathbroker: "The Grove is concluded: felled, the Hunger closed, the holes entered at the rim and none at the heart. What was taken stays taken, and the account is closed. We kept the floor that mattered.",
    ironpalm: "He spoke only hunger, and we answered by choosing where it fed. The old tree is down, and the dark has closed. The holes ring the edge. The middle is still ours.",
    ranger: "Down. Heartwood shut. Holes round the rim, none in the middle, same as planned. Walked off whole.",
    shadowblade: "The dark in his heart has closed its eye, and the mark is down. Every hole at the edge, none in the middle, and I stood in the one good spot as if I'd planned it. Which I had.",
    pyromancer: "He's down! A whole tree, and a sizeable part of him went up by my hand. The dark in his heart shut, the holes ring the room like a crown, and the floor stayed whole, so I stood in the middle and glowed.",
    thermist: "Down, and the dark in his heart has closed. Holes at the rim, the floor whole in the middle. I could dance on it, and did, a little. I am exactly the temperature of the room again, and the room is all still there.",
    driftblade: "The dark in his heart shut when he went down, and I ran the whole middle of the grove just because I could, through where the leaves fell and round the edges where the holes sit, and never landed, and never fell.",
    alembist: "Specimen: felled; the dark in his heartwood closed. Holes: all at the rim, none in the middle. Not a flask cracked. One leaf pressed in my notebook. Label: the Hunger, spent. Pleased, and slightly leafy.",
    threadweaver: "The dark in his heart has been stitched shut. Holes all round the hem, none in the middle, and every one of my stitches walked off it. Sit, all of you; tea after, I think.",
    marionettist: "Final curtain, and the dark in his heart closed like a stage door. The holes ring the boards, none in the middle. (The Doll took her bow in the one clear spot. Ovation.)",
    cleric: "The old tree is at rest, poor thing, and the dark in his heart has closed. Every hole is at the edge, and every one of my dear ones stood on whole ground at the end. I said a prayer for him.",
    grovekeeper: "The oak has gone down, dear, and the dark in him has closed like a wound healing badly. Holes round the edge, none in the middle. I'll plant something in the heartwood when it's cool. Tea, I think. Sit.",
    chronomancer: "The holes are at the rim, as I'd have planned, and did. The middle is whole, and so are we, and the tall one lies down with nothing undone. Proud, I expect, in a moment.",
    tidecaller: "The tree is down and the dark in his heart is still, slack water at last. Holes at the rim, the middle whole. What the Hunger took, it keeps. I sang over him, low. Even slack water turns; the Hunger never did.",
    lanternkeeper: "The dark in his heart closed, and we stood on whole ground. I lit one small lamp where it had been and left it burning. The holes are at the rim, none in the middle.",
    kenseier: "The dark in his heart is shut. Every hole at the edge, the middle whole, and the last stroke was his own. I bowed to the old tree.",
    houndmaster: "Old bull's down, black split in his heart shut. Holes round the rim, none in the middle, dogs fine.",
  },
  moves: {
    'Hunger Seed': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The seed sat over one of the line, never me. They carried it to the rim, or beside an old hole, which swallows it into itself and digs nothing new, and stepped out of the ring the instant it stopped following. I kept the Oak pinned in the middle.",
      gloomsworn: "A seed settled over one of the others. They carried it to the edge, or beside an old hole, which feeds on it instead of opening a new one, and stepped out as the ring set. Better one big hole at the rim than many. I have lain in worse.",
      thresher: "A dark seed hung over one of us, dear, a ring shrinking round it. Off to the edge it went, or beside an old hole, which just takes it and grows a little. Out of the ring the moment it set. Such a tidy way to eat.",
      bloodreaver: "A seed landed on a friend and a ring began to shrink round them! We hauled it to the rim, or next to an old hole that gobbles it down and digs nothing fresh, and out of the ring they went the moment it set.",
      runeguard: "A seed is set over one of us and a ring closes upon it, which is written plainly on the ground. Carry it to the edge, or beside an old hole, which takes it in and digs no new one; then step clear when the ring sets.",
      gorgemaw: "A seed lands on one of my friends like a garnish. Carry it to the rim, or beside an old hole, which will happily finish it and open no new mouth. Step out of the ring when it sets. Waste not.",
      oathbroker: "A seed is assigned to one of the party. It is carried to the edge, or beside an existing hole, which absorbs it without opening another, and the bearer leaves the ring when it sets. The room is kept.",
      ironpalm: "A seed settled on one of us. We carried it to the edge, or beside an old hole, which takes it and opens no new one, and the bearer stepped out as the ring set. Fewer holes, more floor.",
      ranger: "Seed on one of us, ring shrinking. Carry it to the edge, or beside an old hole, which swallows it and digs nothing new. Step out as it sets.",
      shadowblade: "A seed marks one of us. Carry it to the edge, or beside an old hole, which swallows it and opens nothing new, and step out as the ring sets. Fewer holes, fewer places to be caught.",
      pyromancer: "A seed over one of us, and a ring shrinking round it! Carry it to the edge, or beside an old hole, which eats it and digs nothing new. Step out as it sets. Fewer holes, more floor to burn on.",
      thermist: "The seed hung over one of us and the ring closed in, and I ran cold. Walk it to the rim, or alongside a hole already dug, which takes it in so no fresh one opens. Get clear of the ring when it sets. I thawed afterward.",
      driftblade: "A seed rides one of us and I carried it to the edge, or up beside an old hole, which swallows it and digs nothing new, and stepped out of the ring as it set, so there was more floor to run on afterward.",
      alembist: "Specimen: a seed on one of us, a ring closing. Procedure: carry it to the edge, or beside an old hole, which absorbs it without opening another, and step out as it sets. Label: fewer holes, more bench.",
      threadweaver: "A seed rides one of my stitches. Carry it to the edge, or beside an old hole, which takes it in without a new tear, and step out as the ring sets. Fewer holes is fewer places to snag.",
      marionettist: "A seed marks one of the company. Carry it to the wings, or beside an old trapdoor, which swallows it and cuts no new one, and step out of the ring as it sets. (The fewer the holes, the larger the stage.)",
      cleric: "A seed settled on one of the others, and I watched them carry it to the edge, or beside an old hole, which takes it in and opens no new one. They stepped out as the ring set. Every hole not dug is floor kept.",
      grovekeeper: "A seed rides one of my lot. Carry it to the edge, or beside an old hole, which takes it and grows a little but opens no new one, and step out as the ring sets. Fewer holes, more soil.",
      chronomancer: "A seed rides one of us, and the ring closes in a count I knew. Carry it to the edge, or beside an old hole, which takes it in and opens no new one, then step out. I will have said so.",
      tidecaller: "A seed rides one of us. Carry it to the edge, or beside an old hole, which takes it in like an inlet and opens no new one, then step out of the ring as it sets.",
      lanternkeeper: "The seed sat on one of us. I walked the bearer to the edge, or to an old hole, which takes the seed and digs no new one, and held a lamp at the ring's rim so they saw where to step as it set. Floor left whole is floor I can light.",
      kenseier: "One of us wore the seed. The count was long, and on it we walked the bearer to the edge, or to the lip of an old hole, which drinks the seed and opens no new floor. The bearer left the ring on the last beat.",
      houndmaster: "A seed on one of us. Walk it to the edge, or beside an old hole, which swallows it and digs nothing new, then out of the ring as it sets. Fewer holes, more floor for the dogs.",
    } },
    'Inhale': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He drew breath and everything slid toward him. A hole in the way stops the slide at its lip, so I walked the Oak where the road was clean. In the last stretch the petals open the instant the pull ends. The pull bruised every one of us, so the healer had work.",
      gloomsworn: "He breathed in, and the whole room slid toward the split in his chest. A hole in the way stops you at its lip and no further, so mind your next step. Near the end the petals open the moment the pull stops. It bruised us all as it dragged. I have been dragged before.",
      thresher: "He breathes in, dear, and everything leans toward him. A hole in the way holds you at its lip, so do mind the next step. In the last stretch the petals open the moment the pulling stops. It bruises everyone, dear, so the healer will be busy. Be ready, there's a good soul.",
      bloodreaver: "He drew breath and the whole room slid for his heart! A hole in the road stops the slide at the lip, so I only had to mind my feet afterwards. At the end the petals open the moment it stops. It bruised the lot of us! A good pull, spoiled.",
      runeguard: "Dust and light stream toward his heart before the pull, and a careful reader sees it coming. A hole in the road halts you at its lip, so do not step in after. Late in the fight the petals open the moment the pull ends. Every one of us was bruised.",
      gorgemaw: "My host inhaled, and the whole table slid toward his plate. A hole in the way stops a guest at its lip, mercifully. Late in the meal the petals open the instant the sliding stops, so be ready to move again. The slide bruised every guest.",
      oathbroker: "He draws breath and all parties are drawn toward him. A hole lying between stops the party at its lip, not beyond, and none should step in after. Late in the contract the petals open the moment the pull ends. A bruise is levied on all parties.",
      ironpalm: "He drew breath. We were drawn toward him, and a hole in the way held us at its lip. I did not step in after. Late in the fight the petals open the moment the pull ends. The pull bruised us all. Breathe, then move.",
      ranger: "He drew breath. Everything slid toward him. Hole in the line stops you at the lip. Don't step in after. Late in the fight the petals open the moment the pull ends. Bruises everyone.",
      shadowblade: "The mark drew breath and the room slid toward his heart. A hole in the road stops you at its lip, so you simply do not step in after. Late in the fight the petals open the moment the pull ends. The pull bruises everyone.",
      pyromancer: "He inhaled and the whole room slid toward the split in his chest like moths to a lamp! A hole in the way just stops you at its lip, hurrah. Near the end the petals open the second the pull stops. It bruised us all, too.",
      thermist: "He breathed in and the room tipped toward his heart, a scald of dust and light. A hole in the way stops you at its lip, so I never stepped in after. Late in the fight the petals open the instant the pull lets go. Everyone came out bruised.",
      driftblade: "He breathed in and everything slid toward him, and a hole in the way stopped whoever was sliding at its lip, so I slid on where there was none, and late in the fight the petals opened the moment the pull ended and I was already moving, bruised like everyone.",
      alembist: "The drag toward his heart halts at any hole in the way, right at the lip, so do not step in afterward. Late in the fight the petals open the moment the pull lets go. Everyone bruised; poultices all round.",
      threadweaver: "He drew breath and every thread went taut toward his heart. A hole in the way is a stop-knot: you halt at its lip. Late in the fight the petals open the moment the pull goes slack. It frays every thread a little.",
      marionettist: "He inhaled and the company was dragged toward him like a curtain in a draught. A trapdoor in the way stops a player at its lip. Late in the run the petals open the moment the draught drops. (The draught bruises the whole company. Exit, pursued by petals.)",
      cleric: "He drew a great breath and everything slid toward his heart. I told everyone not to step in after, since a hole in the way stops you only at its lip. Late in the fight the petals open the moment it ends. It bruised us all, and I mended as I could.",
      grovekeeper: "He drew breath like a wind pulling in before a storm. A hole in the road stops you at its lip and no further, so mind your step after. Late in the fight the petals open the moment the wind drops. It bruises every root, so I mended them.",
      chronomancer: "The inhale comes just after the light starts streaming. A hole in the way holds you at its lip, so do not step in after. Late in the fight the petals open the moment the pull ends. It bruises everyone, so I healed early; I had warned them before they did it.",
      tidecaller: "He drew breath, a long ebb, and everything went with it. A hole in the way holds you at its lip, a stop in the rip. Late in the fight the petals open the moment the tide stops. It bruised all of us.",
      lanternkeeper: "Dust and leaves and light streamed toward his heart before the pull. A hole in the way stops you at its lip, so I lit the edge and warned them off the step. Late in the fight the petals open the moment it ends. It bruised us all, and I mended.",
      kenseier: "His breath in has a long count, and the light streams toward him before it. A hole in the way stops you at its lip. Late in the fight the petals open on the very count the pull ends. The pull bruises everyone.",
      houndmaster: "Big lad breathed in and the whole lot of us slid toward him. Hole in the way stops you at the lip, and the dogs know not to step in after. Late in the fight the petals open the moment it ends. Bruised the whole pack, mind.",
    } },
    'Fourfold Bloom': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Four petals opened round him, two wide, two narrow, all red. I stood in a gap and held, and when they snapped I stepped round the way they leaned, keeping to the gap, before they snapped again.",
      gloomsworn: "Four petals opened, two broad, two thin, and snapped shut. They turned a little the way they leaned and snapped again. I stood in a gap, walked round with the turn, and stayed in the gap. A gap is generous compared to a coffin.",
      thresher: "Four great petals, dear, two broad and two thin, and every one of them red. They snap shut, then lean and turn a little and snap again. Stand in the gap between, and step round the way they lean. A tidy waltz.",
      bloodreaver: "Four petals, two fat, two thin, all red, all hungry! They snapped, turned a little the way they leaned, and snapped again. I stood in a gap and went round with the turn. The gaps were a gift. Almost shameful.",
      runeguard: "Four petals, two broad and two narrow, and each leans the way it will turn; the lean is the inscription. Stand in a gap, and after the first snap turn with them, keeping the gap, before the second. Thorn, frost, spark, blight, in that order.",
      gorgemaw: "Four petals like four courses, thorn, frost, spark and blight, two broad and two thin, all of them red. They snap, turn a little the way they lean, and snap again. I sat in the gap and turned with the table. Four courses, none for me.",
      oathbroker: "Four petals, two broad and two narrow, all payable in red. They close, turn a little in the direction they lean, and close again. The clause: stand in a gap and move with the turn, keeping to the gap, before the second closing.",
      ironpalm: "Four petals opened, two broad, two narrow. They snapped, leaned, turned a little, and snapped again. I stood in the gap between them, and turned with them. One word, then the next.",
      ranger: "Four petals, two wide, two narrow, all red. They snap, lean, turn a little, snap again. Stood in a gap. Went round with the turn, same gap.",
      shadowblade: "Four petals open round him, which is rude, and snap shut, then lean and turn a little and snap again, which is ruder. I stood in a gap and turned with them, keeping to it.",
      pyromancer: "Four huge petals, thorn and frost and spark and blight, all glowing the colour of trouble! They snapped, leaned, turned a little and snapped again. I stood in a gap and turned with them, and resented every step.",
      thermist: "Four petals, two wide, two narrow, a gap between each. Stand in a gap. They snap, lean, turn a little and snap again, so I swung round with them, fast, and held the gap through the turn. Cold space between the red.",
      driftblade: "Four petals opened round him, two wide, two thin, and snapped, and leaned and turned a little and snapped again, so I stood in a gap and went round with them, through the turn and out the far side, without once coming to rest, the gap keeping step.",
      alembist: "Ailment: four petals, two wide, two narrow, all red; they snap, lean, turn a little, snap again. Remedy: stand in a gap between them, and after the first snap turn with them, keeping to the gap. Label: the petals show the turn.",
      threadweaver: "Four petals opened round him, two broad and two thin, and snapped, then leaned and turned a little and snapped again. I stood in a gap, and turned with them, keeping the seam straight. A sloppy stitch is a bad place to stand.",
      marionettist: "Four petals, two broad, two thin, all red: the stage directions. They snap shut, lean, turn a little, snap again. Stand in a gap, and turn with the set. (Exit through the gap, always. Never through a petal.)",
      cleric: "Four petals opened round him, two broad and two thin, all red. They snapped, leaned, turned a little and snapped again. I stood in a gap and turned with them, and called the others to do likewise.",
      grovekeeper: "Four petals opened, two broad and two thin, like a flower in a bad dream. They close, lean the way they will turn, and close again. I stood in a gap and turned with the bloom. Even a bad flower follows the sun.",
      chronomancer: "Four petals, and the second snap lands late and a little to the side of the first. They lean the way they will turn, so I stood in a gap and turned with them. I was in the gap before I got there.",
      tidecaller: "Four petals opened like a wave rearing, two broad, two thin. They snapped, leaned, turned a little and snapped again. I stood in the gap between them and turned with them, like a boat turning with the swell.",
      lanternkeeper: "Four petals opened round him, two broad and two thin, all red. I stood in a gap, and when they snapped I went round with the turn, still in the gap, and was there before they closed again. Four gaps, four of us.",
      kenseier: "Four petals: two broad, two thin, a stroke, a small turn, and a second stroke. They lean the way they will turn. I stood in a gap and turned with them, on the beat.",
      houndmaster: "Four petals round the big lad, two broad, two thin, all red. They snap, lean, turn a bit, snap again. Stood in a gap and went round with them, dogs on my heels. Slow work, and steady.",
    } },
    'Root Lash': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The roots came for me alone, a heavy lash that holds whoever it touches. I walked him off the holes before it fell, since I couldn't walk after. The rest kept off the ring that follows me.",
      gloomsworn: "The lash was meant for me, and it held whoever stood near. It was one more mark on a long list. I walked him off the holes first, because I'd be standing still after. The rest kept out of my ring.",
      thresher: "Roots whip up around our shield, dear, a heavy blow that holds anyone it touches. The ring follows him, so keep out of it, and he moves the old fellow off the holes before, not after.",
      bloodreaver: "The roots whipped up at me and held me like a lover! Tight and rude. I walked him off the holes first, since I couldn't walk after, and the others stayed out of the ring around me.",
      runeguard: "The roots stir in the ground at the shield's feet before the lash. I lead him off the holes first, for it holds me still; the rest stay out of my ring, and the healer's ward goes up beforehand.",
      gorgemaw: "Roots, whipped into my face, a heavy, woody dish that held me in place while I ate. I walked the host off the holes before the course arrived. The others kept out of the ring around me. Tough, and fibrous.",
      oathbroker: "The lash is a writ upon whoever stands before him: heavy, and binding whoever is near. I move him off the holes before it falls, since I cannot after. The others keep out of the ring about me. Accepted in full.",
      ironpalm: "The roots rose to answer me, a heavy word that holds. Before it fell, I led him off the holes. The others stayed out of the ring around me. I took it, and could not move for a moment.",
      ranger: "Roots whip up at the shield. Heavy, and it holds whoever's near. He walked the beast off the holes first. Rest stayed out of the ring around him.",
      shadowblade: "The mark lashed at our shield with roots that hold, which is pawing. The ring follows him, so I kept out of it, and he'd walked the mark off the holes first.",
      pyromancer: "The roots whipped up at the knight, heavy as a house, and held anything near him! I stayed out of the ring and sulked at a safe distance, still burning; he'd walked the old brute off the holes first.",
      thermist: "Roots at the shield's feet, a heavy lash, a ring that follows him, and anyone inside it is held. I stayed out, ice-steady, while he walked the old tree off the holes first. After the blow is too late.",
      driftblade: "The roots came up at our shield, heavy, and held whoever was in the ring following him, so I stayed out of it and circled while he walked the old tree off the holes before it landed, because nobody walks anywhere once held.",
      alembist: "Observed: a heavy lash on the shield, and roots that hold whoever is near. Walk him off the holes first, keep the rest out of the ring, lay the mending beforehand. A held patient cannot step away from anything, so that part is not optional.",
      threadweaver: "The roots whipped at our knight, a heavy blow that holds whoever is near. The rest of my stitches stayed out of the ring about him, and he walked the old tree off the holes first. A tidy seam.",
      marionettist: "A heavy lash for our shield: roots out of the boards, holding anyone near. The company stayed out of the ring about him, and he walked the star off the trapdoors before the cue. (Exit, held, stage left. Not us.)",
      cleric: "The roots lashed our knight, a heavy blow that holds whoever is close. I laid the light on him before it fell, and the rest kept out of the ring about him. He walked the old tree off the holes first.",
      grovekeeper: "Roots whipped up at the knight like a bad frost, holding anyone near. I'd planted on him before. My lot kept out of the ring about him, and he'd walked the old oak off the holes.",
      chronomancer: "The ring locks, and only then does the lash land, holding whoever is inside. The knight walked him off the holes before it, I mended him early, and the rest stayed out. I knew that last time, which is to say next time.",
      tidecaller: "A heavy lash for our knight, and roots that hold whoever is close, like kelp round an ankle. I raised the water about him before it fell. The rest stayed out of the ring about him, and he walked the old tree off the holes.",
      lanternkeeper: "The roots whipped at our knight, a heavy blow that holds whoever is near. I hung a light on him before it fell and stayed out of the ring. He had walked the old tree off the holes first. One lamp, one blow.",
      kenseier: "The lash is aimed at me, and has a count. I met it as it landed, and the roots held whoever was near, so the rest stayed out of the ring. I walked him off the holes first. An honest blow.",
      houndmaster: "Roots up at the shield-man, and anyone near got held. Dogs kept out of the ring, me too. He walked the old bull off the holes first, since he'd not walk after.",
    } },
    "Hunger's Reach": { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "A hole reaches for me with roots, a bar of them laid through my feet. I stand the old tree far from his holes, and when the bar is painted I take one step sideways. The bar fell behind me. On hard nights a second hole follows through wherever I land.",
      gloomsworn: "Roots lunge from the nearest hole and run through where I stand. A light wound, and a drag toward the pit that stops at its brink. I sidestep and let it find nothing. On hard nights the next pit lunges at my new footing.",
      thresher: "Oh, the nearest hole sends a bar of roots through our shield, dear, and any of us behind him gets whipped along with it. He steps aside; we don't dawdle in the line. A second hole joins on hard nights.",
      bloodreaver: "A hole snatches for me with roots! A bar of them, right through my boots, and a drag to the rim if it lands. I take a stride sideways and let it grab air. Hard nights, another hole grabs where I ended up.",
      runeguard: "A hole inscribes a bar of roots through my feet, and the paint is the margin note that warns of it. One stride aside as it closes, and the party off the ground behind me. On hard nights a second hole writes again where I stand.",
      gorgemaw: "A pit sets a long thin course of roots at me, straight through the plate. One stride aside and the dish goes cold. Anyone behind me gets it too, and gets dragged to the pit's lip. Hard nights bring a second pit, a second helping.",
      oathbroker: "A hole serves a writ of roots: a strip through where I stand, light in penalty, and a forced march toward the pit that halts at its brink. I vacate the strip as it falls. Harder terms add a second hole, served at my new address.",
      ironpalm: "Roots rise from the nearest hole in a bar through my stance. I do not stand in it. One quiet step aside, and the bar closes on nothing; my friends behind me take my place in it if I am careless. Hard nights, a second pit answers.",
      ranger: "Hole to the shield: a bar of roots, from the pit through him and out the back. He sidesteps when it paints. Archers stand wide of that line, always. Hard nights, a second pit shoots at wherever he lands.",
      shadowblade: "The mark's hole throws roots in a bar through our shield, and the shadow behind him is in it too. He steps aside, I am never standing there. It drags the careless to the brink. Hard nights, another hole tries again.",
      pyromancer: "A hole spits roots at the knight in one long bar, and whoever lurks behind him cooks in it too! He steps aside, and I do not lurk. A light hit, and a tug to the lip. On hard nights a second hole spits again.",
      thermist: "A strip of roots from the nearest pit, through the shield and out behind him. I ran hot watching it paint and stood nowhere in his lee. He sidestepped as it landed. Whoever is caught gets a tug to the brink, and hard nights bring the next pit.",
      driftblade: "A pit flung roots down a straight line through the shield and past him, so I kept off the line, wide, and he took one step out of it as it landed, and nothing was dragged anywhere. A second pit tries again on the hard nights.",
      alembist: "The nearest pit throws a bar of roots through the shield and out behind him. One step aside as it paints, and nobody sheltering in his lee. If it lands, the patient is dragged to the brink. Hard nights add a second pit, a second dose.",
      threadweaver: "A pit pulls one taut line of roots through our knight, and my stitches behind him are in it too. He slips the thread, one step aside, and we keep clear of his back. Caught, you are towed to the brink. Hard nights, a second pit pulls again.",
      marionettist: "A trapdoor flings a strip of roots through our lead and out his back, so the chorus behind him is in it too. (He steps off his mark.) A tug to the footlights if it lands. Hard nights, the next trapdoor repeats the cue at his new mark.",
      cleric: "Roots rise from a hole toward our dear knight, in one bar through him and behind. He steps out of it, and I never stand in his shadow. If it lands, a light wound and a pull to the hole's brink. A second hole follows on hard nights.",
      grovekeeper: "Roots out of an empty hole, wanting company: one bar, through the knight and out his back. A sidestep and it finds none. My lot stay out of his wake. Caught, you are hauled to the brink like a pulled weed. Hard nights, another hole wants him too.",
      chronomancer: "I have seen where it falls: a bar of roots from the nearest pit, through the knight, a moment after the paint. He steps off it in time. Whoever idles behind him is hauled to the brink. On hard nights a second pit comes after.",
      tidecaller: "A pit draws roots out like an undertow, a bar from its mouth through our knight and beyond. He wades aside as it comes, and we keep out of his wake. Caught, you are towed to the brink, never in. Hard nights, a second pit pulls at his new place.",
      lanternkeeper: "The nearest pit sends a ribbon of roots through our knight, dark all the way down its length. He steps out of it. I keep my lamp out of his shadow, since it lands there too. Caught, you are led to the brink. Hard nights, another pit follows. One lamp, one lane.",
      kenseier: "The roots have a measure: a bar from the pit, through the shield and out behind, closing on the count after the paint. He moves on it, and so do we, if we stood behind him. Caught, you are drawn to the brink. A second pit answers, on hard nights.",
      houndmaster: "Hole sends roots down a bar through the shield-man, and out the back where the dogs would be. He steps aside. Dogs and me wait wide. Whatever it grabs gets hauled to the lip. Hard nights, a second hole snaps at his new spot.",
    } },
    'Sap Tide': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The sap still reached every one of us, heavier each time. Later the holes filled and spilled over, a band painted round each rim the moment the sap landed. I walked the line off every band onto bare floor, mended or not.",
      gloomsworn: "Amber on all of us, and later each hole brimmed over, a band painted round its rim as the sap landed. I stood clear of every one. Being swallowed once was enough, and I did not enjoy it then either.",
      thresher: "Amber sap, dear, heavier every time, and once the grove is remade each hole brims over after it, a painted band round its rim. Hop out onto clear floor the instant it lands, and be kind to the next patch of ground too.",
      bloodreaver: "Sap on all of us, harder each time! Later the holes brimmed over behind it, a band painted round every rim, and standing in one earns a cheap bruise. I ran out onto clear floor the instant the sap landed, bleeding or not.",
      runeguard: "The sap reaches every one of us; later each hole overflows behind it, and a band is inscribed round its rim as the sap lands. The reading is plain: leave every band for clear floor at once, healed or otherwise, and never shelter on a rim.",
      gorgemaw: "Sap poured over the room, and once the grove is remade each hole spills over like a pot left on the boil, a band painted round its rim. I left the stove for clear floor the moment the sap landed. Scalding, and sticky besides.",
      oathbroker: "The Tide is levied on all parties. After the grove is remade it carries a rider: each hole overflows, a band painted about its rim as the sap lands. The parties vacate every band for open floor immediately, mended or unmended.",
      ironpalm: "The sap spoke to all of us, a little louder each time. Later each hole replied, spilling a painted band round its rim. We stepped off every band onto clear floor, and were mended as we walked.",
      ranger: "Sap hits everyone. Once the grove is remade, each hole spills over as well, painted rim and all. Out of every band, onto open floor. Heal on the move.",
      shadowblade: "Sap for everyone, which is generous. Later every hole brims over after it, a band painted round the rim, and the careless stand in it. I was already stepping onto open floor, away from every rim, when it filled.",
      pyromancer: "Amber on all of us again, a bit heavier! And once the grove is remade every hole spills over after it, a band painted around its rim. I left mid-spell, onto open floor, and finished the thing from there.",
      thermist: "Sap on every one of us, thicker each time, then a rush of heat as every hole brimmed over, a painted band round its rim. I ran clear onto open floor before I felt it, and cooled there.",
      driftblade: "The sap reached everyone, and after it every hole brimmed over, a band painted round its rim the instant the sap landed, so I was off and away across the open floor between the holes, mended on the run, and not stopping.",
      alembist: "Sap on all, heavier each dose. Once the grove is remade each hole boils over after it, a band painted round its rim as the sap lands. Do not wait to be mended: step onto clear floor first, and the poultice can follow.",
      threadweaver: "Sap ran down every thread, heavier each time. Later each hole brimmed over, and I drew my stitches away from all the rims at once, onto clear floor, and mended them there.",
      marionettist: "The set wept amber, a little more each cue. Once the grove is remade the trapdoors brim over too, a band painted round each rim. The company cleared every one for open boards, mended in the wings. (Nobody takes a bow on a rim.)",
      cleric: "Amber sap reached each of us, heavier every time. Later the holes overflowed after it, a band painted round each rim, and I called everyone onto clear floor the moment it landed, mending them on the way. Come away from the rims, friends.",
      grovekeeper: "The sap rises, and once the grove is remade it brims out of every hole, a band painted round each rim like a puddle. My lot left every band for dry ground the moment it landed, and I mended them on the way.",
      chronomancer: "The sap lands on everyone the same, and each hole spills over a heartbeat later, a band painted round the rim. We stepped onto open floor as it landed, and I mended on the move, having done so already.",
      tidecaller: "Amber sap, rising slow and sweet, and once the grove is remade each hole swells over after it, a band painted round its rim like the line a tide leaves. We waded out onto clear floor as it landed, and I healed on the move.",
      lanternkeeper: "Sap reached us all, and then every hole brimmed over. I carried my lamp out to the clear floor between the holes, and the others followed the glow, mended on the way.",
      kenseier: "The sap has no beat a blade can turn. After it, each hole overflows, a band painted round its rim. One step out onto open floor as it lands, and no waiting on the mending. The holes keep honest time.",
      houndmaster: "Sap on the lot of us, dogs too, and once the grove's remade every hole spills over after it, a band painted round the rim. Whistled the dogs out onto clear floor the second it landed, mended on the move.",
    } },
    'Saplings': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Little trees sprouted and set off for the nearest hole. If one reaches it, the hole grows. So the line broke them before they got there, the nearest first, and I kept the Oak still. A hole that grows eats our ground.",
      gloomsworn: "Saplings sprouted and started walking for the holes. One that arrives makes the hole wider. We cut them down on the way, the nearest first. Felling saplings is easier than burying them. I have done both.",
      thresher: "Little saplings pop up, dear, and toddle off to the nearest hole, and the hole gets wider for each one that arrives. Cut them down on the road, the closest to a hole first. Such small appointments.",
      bloodreaver: "Little trees! They marched for the nearest hole, and each one that got there made it wider. I smashed the ones nearest the holes first and then the rest. They barely hurt. Shameful. A fine afternoon's work.",
      runeguard: "The saplings sprout at the edge and walk to the nearest hole, and each that arrives widens it; the sequence is short and easily copied. Break them on the road, the nearest to a hole first.",
      gorgemaw: "Saplings, served in a ring, each walking to the nearest hole like starters carried to a bad table. One that arrives makes the hole bigger. We ate them on the way, nearest first. Bland, but a pleasant crunch.",
      oathbroker: "The saplings are agents of the counterparty. Each walks to the nearest hole and, on arriving, enlarges it. We broke them on the road, the nearest to a hole first, before the breach was widened.",
      ironpalm: "Little trees rose and walked to the holes. Each one that arrived made its hole wider. We broke them on the way, the nearest first, each a short word. The holes stayed small.",
      ranger: "Saplings sprout and walk for the nearest hole. One arrives, hole widens. Shot them down on the road, nearest to a hole first.",
      shadowblade: "Little trees sprouted and went toddling for the holes, and each one that arrives makes its hole wider. I cut them down from behind, the nearest first. Saplings are very poor at watching their backs.",
      pyromancer: "Little trees! I could have wept with joy. They marched for the nearest hole, and each one to arrive made it wider, so I burned the nearest to a hole first and then the rest. A forest, now ash.",
      thermist: "Little trees sprouted and set off for the nearest hole, and each that arrives widens it. I burned a few, the ones nearest a hole first, and enjoyed it more than I should. We broke the rest on the road.",
      driftblade: "Little trees sprang up and ran for the nearest hole and each one that arrived made it wider, so I tore through the lot, nearest first, and round the next and back through, and not a sapling reached the edge.",
      alembist: "Small, soft and headed for the nearest hole, which each one widens on arrival. Break them on the road, the ones nearest a hole first. Weed early. They hardly bruised my knuckles.",
      threadweaver: "Saplings sprouted and walked for the nearest hole, and each one that arrived pulled the hole wider like a loose thread. We snipped them on the road, the nearest first. A sloppy hem, otherwise.",
      marionettist: "Little understudies came on, walking to the nearest trapdoor, and each that arrived made it wider. The company cut them down on their way, the nearest first. (Exit, the understudies. No bows.)",
      cleric: "Little saplings sprouted and set off for the holes, poor things, and each one that arrived made its hole wider. We broke them on the road, the nearest to a hole first. I do not enjoy it. Quick hands, and a quiet apology.",
      grovekeeper: "Saplings, sprouted out of season and heading for the holes. I was sorry to see them cut, but each one that reached a hole widened it. The nearest to a hole went first. Even weeds are seedlings once. Thin early.",
      chronomancer: "The saplings sprout, and some moments later they reach the nearest hole and widen it. We broke them on the road, the nearest first. Quicker next time, which was this time.",
      tidecaller: "Small things, many of them, walking for the nearest hole like water finding a drain, and each one that arrives widens it. We broke them on the way, the nearest first. The shore is worn down this way too.",
      lanternkeeper: "Saplings sprouted round the edge and walked for the nearest hole, and each one that arrived widened it. I lit the ones nearest the holes so we saw them first, and we broke them on the road. Four hands, many saplings.",
      kenseier: "Little trees, each with a short walk to its hole. Each one that arrives widens it. I stepped through the nearest and cut them down, nearest to a hole first. Small work.",
      houndmaster: "Little trees walked off to the nearest hole, and each one that got there made it wider. Put the dogs on them, nearest first, and I broke the rest. Rust would've found every last one, silly lad.",
    } },
    'The Hunger Stirs': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The heartwood split wider, and a light blow reached every one of us as the bar finished. Nothing to turn, nothing to dodge. I kept the line closed up and rode it out. The next move came a few breaths after.",
      gloomsworn: "The tree groaned and the dark in his heart opened wider, and a light blow went through us all at the end. Nothing to step around. I stayed where I stood. I have heard worse noises from the ground.",
      thresher: "The old fellow stirs, dear, and the split in his heart opens a little wider. A light blow reaches us all when it ends, nothing to dodge, so stay together and let the healer fuss. Then the dance picks up again.",
      bloodreaver: "The big one groaned and the black wound gaped, and a light slap reached all of us at the end of it. Not even a scar! I stood still, took it, and went back to hitting him.",
      runeguard: "The bar is named for what it is: the heartwood stirs and parts wider, and a light blow reaches all of us at its close. There is nothing to read and nothing to dodge. We stood near the healer, and the next sign followed.",
      gorgemaw: "My host cleared his throat, the split in his heart yawned, and the course ended with a light blow to every guest. Nothing to dodge, only to swallow. A mere amuse-bouche.",
      oathbroker: "The counterparty announces a fresh clause: the heartwood parts wider and a light penalty falls on every party at its close. No evasion is available or required. The parties stayed together, and proceedings resumed.",
      ironpalm: "He stirred, and the split in his heart widened. A light blow reached us at the end of it. Not a question, only a greeting. We stood together and took it, and then he spoke properly.",
      ranger: "Heartwood splits wider. Bar fills, light hit on everyone at the close. No ground to give up. Stayed put, bow up. Next move came a few breaths on.",
      shadowblade: "The mark stirred, the dark in his heart widened, and a light blow found every one of us at the end, because he cannot be bothered to aim. I held my place and waited for the real move.",
      pyromancer: "The old brute groaned, the split in his heart gaped, and a light blow hit all of us as the bar ended! Nothing to dodge, so I finished my spell straight through it and left the worrying to the healer.",
      thermist: "A low cold groan, and the split in his heart widened. A light blow reached us all at the end, and no step would have helped. I stayed put and breathed, and the heat of the next move came soon after.",
      driftblade: "He groaned and the split in his heart gaped and a light blow caught all of us when the bar ended, with nowhere to run to and nothing to run from, so I stood, a little cross about it, until the next move began.",
      alembist: "Observed: the heartwood parts wider, and a light blow reaches everyone at the end of the bar. Nothing to dodge, nothing to brew in a hurry. Stay topped up and close, and carry on when the next move begins.",
      threadweaver: "The old tree groaned and the split in his heart opened wider, and a light blow ran down every thread at the end. Nothing to dodge, so my stitches stayed close and I mended them after. Then the work resumed.",
      marionettist: "The star stirred, the split in his heart opening, and a light blow reached the whole company on the final beat. No blocking required. We held our marks. (A short overture, then the act proper.)",
      cleric: "The poor old tree groaned and opened wider at the heart, and a light blow fell on each of my friends as the bar finished. No hurry, no harm done. I kept them near me and made sure all were well before the next move.",
      grovekeeper: "The oak groaned like a tree in a gale, and the split in his heart opened wider. A light blow came over us all at the close, nothing to be done but stand. I kept my lot together and let it pass like a first gust.",
      chronomancer: "He stirs, the split widens, and a light blow reaches all of us as the bar ends. Nothing to dodge, so I mended early, and it was done when it arrived. The next move follows soon after, as it did last time.",
      tidecaller: "A low groan, like water moving in a cave, and the split in his heart opened wider. A light blow reached every one of us at the end. Nothing to dodge. I kept the water high and let it break over us together.",
      lanternkeeper: "The split in his heart opened wider, and a light blow reached every one of us as the bar ended. Nothing to dodge. I held my lamp steady and kept us close, and once it passed we were ready for the next move.",
      kenseier: "The tree stirred, and the split in his heart widened. A light blow came at the end with no beat a blade could turn. We stood close and outlasted it. The next stroke followed a few breaths after.",
      houndmaster: "Big lad groaned, the split in his heart opened wider, and a light blow hit the lot of us as the bar finished. No dodging that. Dogs stayed by my knee. Next move came a few breaths on.",
    } },
    'The Swallowing': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The grove tore loose and fell. A blast reached the line first, so we stood close to the healer for it. Then we rode the ground down, no harm in the ride, and the holes came down with us. Petals, sap and the lash followed the landing, in that order.",
      gloomsworn: "The grove tore loose and fell, with us on it. A blast reached all of us first. After that nothing to do but ride the pieces down. The holes came with us, so I knew where the ground was. Petals opened soon after the landing, then sap and the lash. I have fallen further.",
      thresher: "Down goes the whole grove, dear, with us on it! A blast reaches everyone first, so stand close to the healer. Then ride the pieces down, nothing to dodge, and mind the holes, for they come down with us. Petals, sap and a lash follow the landing, dear.",
      bloodreaver: "The whole grove tore loose and fell, and we rode it down! A blast hit all of us first, and then nothing, just falling. A lovely drop. The holes fell with us, so I knew where not to stand. Petals, sap and the lash came right after.",
      runeguard: "A blast crosses the whole grove, and then the ground tears loose and falls. Gather near the healer for the blast, then ride the pieces down; there is nothing to dodge. The holes come with us, and petals, a sap and a lash follow the landing.",
      gorgemaw: "My host swallowed the whole grove, and us with it. A blast first, shared by all, then a long ride down on the pieces, nothing to chew on. The holes came with us, like bones in the stew. Petals for starter, sap and lash for pudding.",
      oathbroker: "The grove is surrendered: a blast upon all parties, then the ground falls away and we ride it down. No penalty attaches to the ride. The holes are carried with us. The petals, the sap and the lash fall due, in that order, after the landing.",
      ironpalm: "He spoke once to everyone, and the grove fell. We took the blast together, then rode the pieces down without a blow. The holes came with us. When we landed, more words followed: petals, sap and lash.",
      ranger: "Blast on everyone. Then the grove tore loose and fell. Rode the pieces down. Nothing to dodge. Holes came with us. Petals, sap, lash right after the landing.",
      shadowblade: "The mark swallowed the grove, and us with it, which seems a little grabby. A blast reaches everyone first, so I stood close to the healer, then rode the pieces down in comfort. The holes came with us. Petals, sap and a lash after the landing.",
      pyromancer: "The whole grove fell! A blast hit everyone first, and then nothing to dodge, just a long glorious ride on the pieces. The holes came with us, glowing, rude. Petals, sap and the lash right after we landed.",
      thermist: "A blast on everyone first, so I stood close to the healer and took it with the rest. Then the whole grove fell and we rode the pieces down, nothing to dodge, the holes falling with us. Petals, sap and the lash came right after the landing, and I was shivering with something like joy.",
      driftblade: "A blast hit everyone first and then the grove tore loose and fell and I rode a piece of it down like a gust, hopping through the others, and the holes fell with us so I knew where not to land, and the petals and the sap and the lash came right after.",
      alembist: "Brace for the blast close to the healer, then ride the grove down in pieces; nothing to dodge on the way. The holes fall with us, and petals, sap and the lash follow the landing in that order. Two shocks in a row, and I braced for both.",
      threadweaver: "A blast reached every thread at once, and then the grove tore loose and fell, with us on it. I kept my stitches close for the blast and rode the pieces down. The holes came along. Petals, a sap and a lash right after.",
      marionettist: "Trapdoors, all of them! A blast across the house, and the whole stage falls away beneath us. The company gathered near the healer for the blast, then rode the pieces down. (The trapdoors fell with us. Petals, then sap, then lash, at the landing.)",
      cleric: "The grove gave way beneath us, poor thing, and a blast went through us first. I drew everyone in close so one prayer could cover them, and we rode the pieces down together. The holes fell too. After we landed came the petals, then the sap, then the lash.",
      grovekeeper: "The whole grove tore loose and fell with us in it, like a field turned over by a flood. A blast first, shared by all, then a long fall on the pieces. The holes came with us. Petals, sap and a lash after the landing.",
      chronomancer: "A blast first, and the grove falls on its heels. We stood close to the healer for the blast and rode the pieces down, holes and all. Petals, then sap and the lash, follow the landing in order. Ready, by the time I was asked.",
      tidecaller: "The blast broke over every one of us, and then the whole grove tore loose and fell, like a shelf of ice into the sea. We rode the pieces down. The holes came with us. Petals, sap and lash on the landing.",
      lanternkeeper: "A blast reached us all, and then the grove fell. We stood close for the blast and I lit our way down. The holes came with us, so I lit the edge of each. Petals, sap and the lash were waiting at the landing.",
      kenseier: "A blast with no beat a blade could turn, and then the grove fell with us on it. We rode the pieces down, and the holes came with us. Petals, sap and the lash a few beats after the landing. A strange teacher.",
      houndmaster: "Whole grove tore loose and fell with us on it, dogs too. A blast first, then a long ride down on the pieces, nothing to do but hold on. Holes fell with us. Petals, sap and the lash right after we landed.",
    } },
    'The Grove Remade': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He rose out of the fall wearing four limbs, grown from the monsters we've beaten: the butcher's cleaver, the Broodmother's roots, the Storm's knot, the King's lantern. Each has its own bar. Break the one you can't stand, in whatever order, and its blow is gone.",
      gloomsworn: "He rose in a new shape, wearing four limbs grown from things we had killed. A cleaver, roots, a storm, a lantern. Each can be broken on its own, and a broken limb takes its blow with it. Old faces, mostly. I have been among the dead before.",
      thresher: "He rises again, dear, wearing four limbs grown from old guests of ours: the butcher's cleaver, the Broodmother's roots, the Storm's knot, the King's lantern. Each has its own bar, and a broken one takes its trick with it. Choose the order.",
      bloodreaver: "He rose wearing four limbs grown from monsters I've bled! The butcher's cleaver, the spider's roots, the Storm's knot, the King's lantern! Each has its own bar, and a broken one takes its blow with it. Break them in whatever order you like.",
      runeguard: "He returns bearing four limbs grown from the beaten, and I recognise the hands: the Butcher's cleaver, the Broodmother's roots, the Storm's knot, the Hollow King's lantern. Break any limb on its own bar and its move leaves with it. We may choose the order.",
      gorgemaw: "My host rose again wearing four dishes I had already eaten: Grubnak's cleaver, the Broodmother's roots, the Storm's knot, the Hollow King's lantern. Each has its own bar, and a limb broken is a course struck from the menu. The order is ours. Leftovers.",
      oathbroker: "The counterparty emerged with four limbs, grown from parties we have already settled: the Butcher, the Broodmother, the Storm, the Hollow King. Each limb has its own bar. A limb broken forfeits its blow. The order of settlement is ours.",
      ironpalm: "He rose, wearing four old voices: the butcher's cleaver, the Broodmother's roots, the Storm's knot, the King's lantern. Each limb has its own bar. Break one, and its voice is gone. The order is ours to choose.",
      ranger: "He rose wearing four limbs from game I'd already taken. Butcher's cleaver. Broodmother's roots. Storm's knot. King's lantern. Each has its own bar. Break one, its blow's gone. Pick the order.",
      shadowblade: "The mark returned wearing four limbs, stolen from old marks of ours: the butcher's cleaver, the spider's roots, the Storm's knot, the King's lantern. Each has its own bar, and a broken limb takes its blow with it. We chose the order.",
      pyromancer: "A bigger shape, and four limbs to go with it, every one a monster I remember burning: cleaver, roots, storm, lantern! Each has a bar of its own, and when a limb breaks its blow goes with it. I picked the loudest to start.",
      thermist: "Four limbs, grown from monsters we'd beaten: cleaver, roots, storm and lantern, each with a bar of its own. Break a limb and its blow goes with it, in any order we like. I went for the hottest-looking one first.",
      driftblade: "He rose wearing four limbs, the butcher's cleaver and the spider's roots and the Storm's knot and the King's lantern, each with its own bar, and I swept through them in whatever order suited, and every one broken took its blow with it.",
      alembist: "Second form: four limbs, from the butcher, the Broodmother, the Storm and the King. Each has its own bar, and a broken limb takes its blow with it. The order is ours, so I started with whichever hurt most.",
      threadweaver: "He rose wearing four limbs, sewn on from monsters we'd beaten: the butcher's cleaver, the spider's roots, the Storm's knot, the King's lantern. Each has its own bar, and a limb snipped off takes its trick with it. The order is ours.",
      marionettist: "Second act! The star returns wearing four costumes from old productions: the butcher's cleaver, the spider's roots, the Storm's knot, the King's lantern. Each limb has its own bar, and a broken one takes its scene with it. (We chose the order.)",
      cleric: "He rose again wearing four limbs, grown from the poor creatures we put to rest. The butcher's cleaver, the Broodmother's roots, the Storm's knot, the King's lantern. Each has its own bar, and a broken limb takes its blow away. The order is ours.",
      grovekeeper: "He rose grafted, dear: four limbs from monsters we'd put in the ground. The butcher's cleaver, the Broodmother's roots, the Storm's knot, the King's lantern. Each has its own bar. Prune one and its blow is gone. We chose the order.",
      chronomancer: "The limbs were all familiar, which was the joke: the butcher's cleaver, the Broodmother's roots, the Storm's knot, the King's lantern, each with its own bar. A broken limb loses its blow, and I will have chosen the order.",
      tidecaller: "Up from the deep he came, hung with four old wrecks: a cleaver, a tangle of roots, a storm knotted up like rope, and a lantern. Each limb has a bar of its own, and when one breaks its blow sinks with it. We took them in whatever order the water suggested.",
      lanternkeeper: "He rose wearing four limbs, and one of them is the King's own lantern, a pale one. Cleaver, roots, storm, lantern: each from a monster we'd put out. Each has its own bar. A broken limb takes its blow away. Four limbs, four turns.",
      kenseier: "Four limbs, grown from teachers we have already met: the butcher's cleaver, the Broodmother's roots, the Storm's knot, the King's lantern. Each has its own bar. A limb broken takes its stroke with it. The order is ours.",
      houndmaster: "He rose with four limbs sewn on, all from game we'd taken: butcher's cleaver, spider's roots, the Storm's knot, the King's lantern. Each has its own bar. Break one, its blow's gone. Any order suits. Dogs knew them all.",
    } },
    'Cleaver Bough': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "A blue ring settled on one of the line, and it wants us all in it, since it's too heavy for one. We stood together. Then a chop came down the line through the ring, so we stepped off it the moment the blow landed. It widens any hole it crosses.",
      gloomsworn: "A blue ring on one of us. The blow is too heavy for one, so we stood in it together. Then the cleaver came down the line from the bough through where we stood, so we stepped aside. I have been cut worse. It widens the holes it crosses. Not by a tree.",
      thresher: "A blue ring on one of us, dear, and everyone is meant to stand in it, for the blow is too heavy to carry alone. Then the cleaver chops down the line from the bough through where we stood. Step off it! It widens any hole it crosses, dear.",
      bloodreaver: "The butcher's old cleaver on a branch! A blue ring on one of us, and we all piled in to take it together, a heavy one! Then a chop followed down the line from the bough through us, and we got off it. It fattens any hole it crosses. I miss the butcher's cooking.",
      runeguard: "A blue ring is painted on one of us before the swing, and the blow is too heavy for one, so we gather in it together. Then step off the line the chop paints from the bough through the ring. That line widens any hole it crosses.",
      gorgemaw: "Grubnak's cleaver, grown on a branch. A blue ring over one guest, and we all sat in it to share the portion, too heavy for one. Then the cleaver fell along the line from the bough through where we sat. We stepped off. Clumsy carving. It widened the holes it crossed. Overcooked.",
      oathbroker: "A blue ring is entered against one of us, and the clause is that all of us stand in it: it is too heavy for one. Thereafter the cleaver falls along the line from the bough through the ring. The parties vacate the line. Holes crossed by the line are enlarged.",
      ironpalm: "A blue ring on one of us. A heavy word, too heavy to say alone, so we stood in it together. Then the cleaver answered, along the line from the bough through the ring. We stepped aside, and it fell on empty ground. It widens any hole it crosses.",
      ranger: "Blue ring on one of us. Too heavy alone, so all of us in it. Then a chop down the line from the bough through the ring. Off the line. Chop widens holes it crosses.",
      shadowblade: "A blue ring over one of us, and we all crowded in, since the blow is too heavy for one. The chop follows down the line from the bough through the ring, which is predictable of him. I was off the line. It widens holes it crosses.",
      pyromancer: "A blue ring over a friend's head and a cleaver the size of a barn! We all piled into the ring, because it's too heavy for one, and then ran off the line the chop paints from the bough through it. The chop widens any hole it crosses.",
      thermist: "A blue ring on one of us, a blow too heavy for one, so all of us crowded in and shared it. Then the cleaver chopped down the line from the bough through the ring. I was off the line before it fell, cold air at my heels. Holes it crosses grow.",
      driftblade: "A blue ring on one of us and everyone ran into it, because the blow is too heavy for one, and then out again sideways, off the line the chop paints from the bough through the ring, and the cleaver came down on bare floor, and any hole it crossed grew.",
      alembist: "The blue ring wants everyone standing in it, being too heavy for one patient. Then the chop comes down the line through the ring from the bough, so out of it straight away. Holes it crosses are widened. Share the dose, then scatter.",
      threadweaver: "A blue ring over one of my stitches, and the blow was too heavy for one, so I drew every stitch into it. Then we all stepped off the line the chop painted from the bough through the ring. Well sewn. Holes it crossed frayed wider.",
      marionettist: "A blue mark on one of the company, and the whole cast gathered on it, too heavy for a solo. Then the cleaver chopped down the line from the bough through the mark. (Exit, all, stage left. Trapdoors in the line widen.)",
      cleric: "A blue ring settled over one of my friends, a blow too heavy for one, so we gathered in it together and I mended them before it fell. Then the cleaver came down the line through the ring, and we were already off it. It widened the holes it crossed.",
      grovekeeper: "A blue ring on one of my lot. Too heavy for one root to bear, so we all stood in it. I planted the cure first. Then the chop came down the line from the bough through the ring, and we'd stepped aside. A hole it crosses grows wider.",
      chronomancer: "The ring comes first, and the chop trails it, down the line from the bough through where we stood. We gathered in the ring, then stepped off the line. Holes it crosses widen. I had moved before it was cast.",
      tidecaller: "A blue ring on one of us, a swell too heavy for one. We stood in it together and I brought the water up on them beforehand. Then the cleaver came down the line from the bough through the ring, and we'd ebbed aside. Holes in its path widen.",
      lanternkeeper: "A blue ring over one of us, too heavy for one. I hung a light in the ring's middle and we all stood in it. Then the cleaver chopped down the line through the ring, and we were already off it. It widens holes in its path.",
      kenseier: "A blue ring on one of us, and a heavy stroke: the cleaver. We shared it in the ring. A second stroke follows along the line from the bough through the ring, with a count I could hear. We stepped aside. Honest. It widens the holes it crosses.",
      houndmaster: "Big blue ring on one of us. Too heavy for one, so in we went, dogs too. Then the chop came down the line from the bough through the ring. Stepped off it. Like a butcher's block. Widens any hole it crosses.",
    } },
    'Brood Roots': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Five strands painted from the limb. We stood in a gap, then the strands closed and turned half a gap and closed again, so the middle of our gap was a strand. I stood at the edge, near the strand that had just closed, and stepped onto its line.",
      gloomsworn: "Five strands, and they close twice. The second set turns half a gap, so the middle of every gap becomes a strand. I stood at a gap's edge, and stepped onto the line the first strand had struck. Held in place is a skill of mine. Once is enough.",
      thresher: "Five strands, dear, and they close twice, the second set turned half a gap, so the middle of every gap is wrong the second time. Stand by a strand's edge and step onto where it fell.",
      bloodreaver: "Five strands, and they close twice! The second set turns half a gap, so the middle of the gap is a strand! I stood by the edge of the first, and stepped onto the ground it had struck. Barely a scratch, if you're quick.",
      runeguard: "Five strands close twice, the second set turned half a gap; the middle of a gap therefore fails on the second. Stand at a strand's edge, and step onto the line it has just struck.",
      gorgemaw: "Five strands, like a spider's tablecloth, and a second cloth laid over the first, turned half a gap. The middle of a gap is no safe seat the second time. I sat by a strand and shifted onto where it fell. Sticky, but not for me.",
      oathbroker: "Five strands, entered twice: the second set turned by half a gap, voiding the middle of every gap. The parties stood at a strand's edge and moved onto the ground it had struck. That ground is spent.",
      ironpalm: "Five strands, and they spoke twice. The second set turned half a gap, so the middle of the gap was a strand. We stood beside a strand, and stepped onto its line when it had struck. Still ground, then.",
      ranger: "Five strands, closing twice. Second set turns half a gap, so the gap's middle goes bad. Stood at a strand's edge, stepped onto the line it had struck.",
      shadowblade: "Five strands, and they close twice, the second turned half a gap. The middle of the gap is a trap the second time. I stood by a strand and slipped onto the ground it had already struck.",
      pyromancer: "Five strands, and they close twice, and the second set turns half a gap! The middle of a gap is bad the second time! I stood by a strand's edge, then stepped onto the ground it had burned, still casting.",
      thermist: "Five strands, closing twice. The second set sits half a gap round from the first, so the middle of every gap is cursed. Stand by a strand's edge, and when it strikes, step onto its line. Struck ground stays quiet. Steady hands.",
      driftblade: "Five strands and then they closed twice and turned half a gap the second time so the middle of the gap was a strand, so I stood at the edge of one and stepped onto the line it had struck as it lifted, and I never landed.",
      alembist: "Five strands close twice, and the second set is turned half a gap, so the middle of a gap is poison the second time. Stand at a strand's edge and step onto the line it just struck; struck ground is safe. A sticky business, done twice.",
      threadweaver: "Five strands, and the second set was laid turned half a gap, which is shoddy work: the middle of a gap fell under a new strand. My stitches stood by an old strand and stepped onto its line once it had struck.",
      marionettist: "Five strands, a second set of strings strung half a gap along, so the middle of each gap was no place for an actor. The company stood by a strand, and crossed onto its line after it struck. (Spent strings are free.)",
      cleric: "Five strands, and a second set turned half a gap, so the middle of each gap is no safe place. I called my dears to a strand's edge, and onto its line once it had closed.",
      grovekeeper: "Five strands, thrown like runners, and they close twice, the second set turned half a gap. The middle of a gap is bare. My lot stood at a strand's edge and stepped onto the line it had struck. Runners, once cut, are spent.",
      chronomancer: "Five strands, closing twice, the second set turned half a gap. The middle of a gap spoils. I stood at a strand's edge, then stepped onto the line it had struck. The second is painted as the first closes.",
      tidecaller: "Five strands, like nets cast twice, the second turned half a gap. The middle of a gap is a bad current. We stood at the edge of a strand and drifted onto the line it had struck. Struck water is calm.",
      lanternkeeper: "Five strands, closing twice, the second set turned half a gap, so the middle of every gap went dark. We stood by a strand, and my light moved onto the line it had struck. Struck ground is quiet.",
      kenseier: "Five strands, and a second stroke turned half a gap from the first. The middle of the gap is the wrong place at the wrong beat. I stood by a strand and stepped onto its line once it struck.",
      houndmaster: "Five strands, closing twice, second set turned half a gap. Middle of a gap goes bad the second time. Stood us by a strand's edge, then onto the line it struck. Rust learned it quick.",
    } },
    'Storm Knot': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Three rings painted where three of us stood, and they followed us. I dragged mine clear of the others, and when it stopped following I walked out of it, and kept out of theirs. A standing man is an easy mark.",
      gloomsworn: "Three rods, each in a ring that followed one of us until the last moment. I led mine away from the others, then stepped out as it set. The Storm never struck me the same way twice. I would remember.",
      thresher: "Three rings, dear, each following someone about like a puppy until the last moment, a rod at the heart. Lead yours away from the others, and step out the moment it stops following. Such a tidy storm.",
      bloodreaver: "Three rods, three rings, each following one of us about! Lead yours away from the rest, and walk out the moment it stops. A beautiful bolt, and I almost wanted it. I'd have had a scar worth showing, had I stayed.",
      runeguard: "Three rings follow three of us, a rod at each centre, until the last moment. Lead yours away from the others, and leave it the moment it stops following.",
      gorgemaw: "The Storm's trick again, three rods in three rings, each following its guest about the room until the last course. I carried mine away from the others, then got up from it. Charred, and too salty.",
      oathbroker: "Three rods are entered, each ring following its party until the last moment. The clause: lead yours clear of the others, and vacate it the instant it ceases to follow. The Storm's terms, borrowed.",
      ironpalm: "Three rings follow three of us, a rod at each heart. We led ours away from the others and stepped out when they stopped following. The Storm speaks three times, and finds a moving man.",
      ranger: "Three rings, each following one of us, rod at the centre. Led mine off from the others. When it stopped following, stepped out.",
      shadowblade: "Three rings, each following one of us, rods in the middle, which is rude. I led mine away from the others, then left it as it set. The lightning struck floor.",
      pyromancer: "Three rods of lightning, each in a ring that followed one of us about, and it was glorious! I led mine away from the others, then stepped out the moment it stopped. Cast on.",
      thermist: "Three rings follow three of us, a rod at each centre. I ran mine out from the others, the air crackling on my skin, and stepped out the moment it stopped following. Lightning is my kind of weather, just not on me.",
      driftblade: "Three rings and each followed one of us about, a rod at its heart, so I ran mine out away from the others and when it stopped following I slipped out of it through the gap, and the lightning struck only floor.",
      alembist: "Three rods in three rings, each ring following its patient until the last breath. Lead yours away from the others and step out the moment it stops. I kept the glassware well clear of my own ring.",
      threadweaver: "Three rings, each following a thread of mine, a rod at each centre. I led mine clear of the others and stepped out when it set. A rod is a poor needle, and a worse leash.",
      marionettist: "Three rings followed three of the company about the stage, a rod at each centre: the Storm's old cue. Lead yours away from the others, then exit when it stops. (The lightning struck an empty mark. Reviews: shocked.)",
      cleric: "Three rods came down in rings that followed three of our friends about. Each led theirs away from the others and stepped out the moment it stopped following. Nobody stood still.",
      grovekeeper: "The Storm's knot: three rods, each ring following someone about until the last moment. I led mine away from my lot and stepped out as it set. Lightning in a tree is the oldest story, so I moved.",
      chronomancer: "The rings follow their hero until the last moment, and the lightning trails them by a heartbeat. Everyone led theirs away from the others and stepped out as it set. I moved first, eventually.",
      tidecaller: "Three rings followed three of us, a rod at each heart, like a squall chasing its ship. Lead yours away from the others, and step out the moment it stops following. The Storm used to be weather.",
      lanternkeeper: "Three rods came down in rings that followed three of us about. I carried my light away from the others, and stepped out the moment the ring stopped following. Three rods, four of us.",
      kenseier: "Three rings, a rod in each, following three of us. Three strokes, and one long beat before them. I led mine from the others and stepped out when it stopped following. The Storm taught me this once.",
      houndmaster: "Three rings following three of us about, a rod in each. Led mine away from the others and the dogs, and stepped out when it stopped following. Never liked lightning in a field.",
    } },
    'Barrow Lantern': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "A pale light settled on one of us and burst on whoever stood near. Then it passed to the farthest of us, and burst again. So the marked one stood apart from the line, and the line kept off. When it jumped, the new one walked clear.",
      gloomsworn: "A pale lantern light marks one of us, and bursts around them. Then it passes to whoever stands farthest away. We kept apart from the marked one and walked clear when it passed. The lantern is the King's. I knew him by his light.",
      thresher: "A pale light on one of us, dear, and it bursts around them, then passes to whoever stands farthest away. Keep away from the marked one, and when it comes to you, go off alone. A grave-lantern and good manners.",
      bloodreaver: "A pale light settled on one of us and burst around them! Then it jumped to whoever was farthest away, and burst again. The marked one stayed clear of the others, and the rest kept off. The King's own lantern, and I can't stop smiling.",
      runeguard: "A pale lantern mark settles on one of us. It bursts around them, then passes to the one farthest from them, on a beat. The marked one stands apart; the rest keep clear; the next to be marked walks away.",
      gorgemaw: "The Hollow King's lantern, grown on a branch: a pale glow on one guest, bursting around them, then passed on to the guest sitting farthest away. The marked one sat apart; the rest kept their plates clear. A cold, funereal dish.",
      oathbroker: "The Barrow's lantern is a writ passed hand to hand. It marks one party, discharges around them, and transfers to the party farthest away, on a beat. The marked party stands apart; the rest keep clear; the next vacates at once.",
      ironpalm: "A pale light settled on one of us, and spoke, and passed to the farthest of us, and spoke again. Whoever was marked stood apart, and the rest kept off. When it came to them, they walked away. A call, then a call.",
      ranger: "Pale light on one of us. Bursts round them, then passes to whoever's farthest. Marked one stands apart. Rest keep off. When it jumps, the new one clears out.",
      shadowblade: "A pale light marks one of us, which is the opposite of my profession. It bursts around them and passes to whoever is farthest away. The marked one stood apart; I kept off. It passed me by.",
      pyromancer: "A pale grave-light on one of us, bursting around them and then passing to whoever was farthest away! I resented the competition. The marked one stood apart, the rest kept off, and the new one went away from everyone.",
      thermist: "A pale light settled on one of us, burst around them, and passed to the farthest. Marked: stand apart. The rest keep off. When it came to me I went cold and walked clear of everyone until it burst on bare floor.",
      driftblade: "A pale light settled on one of us and burst around them and then jumped to whoever was farthest away, so the marked one ran off by themselves, and the rest kept off, and when it came to me I ran off round the edge, through nobody.",
      alembist: "A pale light bursts around one of us, then passes to whoever stands farthest, on a beat. Marked, stand apart; everyone else, keep off; when it passes to you, walk away from the rest. Infectious, but only if you crowd.",
      threadweaver: "A pale light marked one of my stitches and burst around them, then jumped to whoever stood farthest. The marked one drew the thread away from the rest, and we kept off. When it jumped, the new one did the same.",
      marionettist: "A pale spotlight on one of the company: it bursts around the star, then passes to the actor farthest from them. The marked player stands apart, the rest keep off, and the spot moves on. (Exit the spotlight, pursued by manners.)",
      cleric: "A pale light marked one of my friends, burst around them, and passed to the farthest. The marked one stood apart, and the rest kept off, and when it came to the next they went off alone. I mended each before the burst.",
      grovekeeper: "The pale lantern marks one of my lot, bursts around them, and passes to whoever stands farthest. The marked one stands apart and the rest keep clear, like seed in a row. When it jumps to you, move away.",
      chronomancer: "A pale light marks one of us, and on the beat it passes to the farthest. The marked one stood apart, and the rest kept off. When it passed, the new one walked away. I was the last one marked, by my count; by theirs, the first.",
      tidecaller: "A pale glow came to rest on one of us like a lantern left on the shingle, and burst around them. Then it drifted to whoever stood farthest out. The marked one waited alone, the rest stood back, and when it reached the next they walked out alone.",
      lanternkeeper: "The Hollow King's lantern, hung on a branch. Its pale light marks one of us, bursts around them, then passes to whoever stands farthest. I kept apart from the others when it was mine. Three bursts, four of us, none caught.",
      kenseier: "A pale light marks one of us, and the burst has a count. It passes to the farthest on the beat. The marked one stood apart, the rest kept off, and the next walked clear. A relay, not a duel.",
      houndmaster: "Pale light on one of us, bursts round them, then jumps to whoever's farthest. Marked one stands apart. The rest of us kept off, hounds included. When it jumped to me I walked off alone.",
    } },
    'Starved': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The Oak named himself Starved, and the heartwood split wider still. A light blow reached the whole line when the bar ended. Nothing to turn. What follows is the pull, so I let the healer top us up before it.",
      gloomsworn: "He named himself Starved, and the dark in his heart opened again. A light blow took all of us at the end of it. I have been starved too. Be ready for the pull that comes straight after.",
      thresher: "Starved, dear, is what his last stretch is called, and the split in his heart opens wider still. A light blow for everyone when it ends, nothing to dodge. Gather your breath: the great pull comes right after.",
      bloodreaver: "Starved! He gaped wider than ever and cuffed all of us lightly at the close. I grinned. The real hurt is the pull straight after, so I stayed close and let the healer top me up first.",
      runeguard: "He is named Starved, and the heartwood splits wider than before; a light blow reaches every one of us at its close. Nothing to dodge. The sign that follows is the pull, and we are better mended before it than after.",
      gorgemaw: "My host called the last course Starved, which I thought rich. A light blow for every guest at the end, and the heartwood gaped wider. The great pull comes as the plates are cleared, so I ate and braced.",
      oathbroker: "The final instalment is styled Starved: the heartwood parts wider and a light penalty falls on all parties at its close. No evasion required. The pull follows immediately, so the parties are best mended before it.",
      ironpalm: "He called himself Starved, and the split widened again. A light blow reached us at the end of the word. Then the pull came, a longer word. We were mended before it, and met it standing.",
      ranger: "Starved. Split in the heartwood opens wide again. Light hit on all of us at the close, no way round it. The pull comes straight on its heels, so get healed first.",
      shadowblade: "The mark announced itself Starved, which I do not doubt. The split in his heart opened wider and a light blow found us all at the end. The pull follows at once, and I wanted my wounds mended before it.",
      pyromancer: "Starved! The split in his heart yawned wider than ever, and a light blow caught all of us at the end, nothing to dodge. The pull comes right after, and I wanted my wounds mended and my feet planted for it.",
      thermist: "Starved, he called it, and I ran cold. The heartwood split wider, and a light blow reached us all at the end. The pull follows straight after, hot on its heels, so I stood close to the healer.",
      driftblade: "Starved, the bar said, and his heart gaped wider and a light blow caught us all at the end with nothing to run from, but the pull comes straight after and that I could see coming, so I got mended and stood ready to be dragged.",
      alembist: "Observed: he names himself Starved, the heartwood splits wider, and a light blow reaches all at the close. No evasion needed. The pull follows at once, so take the dose before it, not after.",
      threadweaver: "He called himself Starved and the split in his heart gaped wider, and a light blow ran down every thread at the close. The great haul comes straight after, so I tied my stitches up and got them mended first.",
      marionettist: "The star gave the final scene its title: Starved. The split in his heart opened wide and a light blow reached the whole company at the close. (No blocking.) The great pull follows at once, so mend the company in the wings.",
      cleric: "He named himself Starved, poor soul, and the split in his heart opened wider. A light blow reached us all at the end. The great pull follows immediately, so I mended everyone before it, not after.",
      grovekeeper: "Starved, he called himself, and the split in his heart opened like a frost crack. A light blow reached every one of my lot at the end. The pull follows quick as a squall, so I planted the mending first.",
      chronomancer: "Starved is the name of the last stretch, and the split opens wider, and a light blow lands on all of us at its close. The pull follows straight after, so I mended before it arrived, which is how I remember it.",
      tidecaller: "Starved, said the bar, and the split in his heart opened like a river mouth. It broke over every one of us at the last. The great pull comes straight on its heels, so I raised the water before it.",
      lanternkeeper: "He called himself Starved, and the split in his heart opened wider. A light blow reached us all as the bar ended. The pull follows at once, so I topped up every wick I could first, and we stood close.",
      kenseier: "Starved: the last lesson, and the heartwood splits wider. A light blow at the end, no beat a blade could turn. The pull follows on the next stroke, so we were mended before it came.",
      houndmaster: "Starved, the big lad called himself, and the split in his heart opened wider yet. Light blow on the lot of us at the end. The pull comes right on its heels, so I got healed first and held the dogs close.",
    } },
    'Nothing Left': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He drew in the whole grove, and the blow reached all of us. Nothing left to stand on, and no shield turns that. The limbs were still standing when it came. Next time we break them faster and finish him sooner.",
      gloomsworn: "He took everything: the grove, the floor, us. The watch ends when it ends, and we ran past it. We were too slow. I know when a post is lost. Next time, quicker work on the limbs.",
      thresher: "At the last he stops waiting, dear, and takes everything there is. Nothing is tidy after that. Be done before it, and break the limbs quickly. His name was almost off the list.",
      bloodreaver: "He took everything at once and nobody got up from it! Too slow, all of us; we lingered on the limbs. Break them fast, then hit him harder.",
      runeguard: "The grove goes quiet, and then Nothing Left. There is no answer to it, save to finish him before. Break the limbs without delay, and do not waste an opening.",
      gorgemaw: "In the end my host ate the lot, the floor, the grove and us. We dawdled over the limbs, a poor diner's habit. Eat faster, break the limbs quicker, and finish him before the plates are cleared.",
      oathbroker: "Nothing Left falls due at an hour fixed in advance. The performance was incomplete and the forfeit fell on every party. The limbs were broken too slowly. Break them faster.",
      ironpalm: "He ran out of patience, and took everything, and none of us was left to answer. We were too slow with the limbs. Next time, quicker.",
      ranger: "Whole grove goes. Nothing left. Spent too long on the limbs. Break them fast, be done before. Next time the limbs go first.",
      shadowblade: "He took everything, the floor and the grove and all of us, and not even I could hide. We spent too long on the limbs. Be quick, be done, be gone. Rude of it to keep such strict time.",
      pyromancer: "Everything went! The floor, the grove, my best spell, all of us at once, and I had wasted time on the small limbs. Be quicker. Burn harder, break the limbs fast, and finish him first.",
      thermist: "He took everything, the floor, the grove, all of us, and I went cold before the heat of it even arrived. We lingered on the limbs. Break them fast, burn him down early, and be done before he gathers it.",
      driftblade: "He took everything and there was nowhere to run, and I ran anyway, and it caught me, because we were slow with the limbs and Nothing Left does not wait, and every limb could have been fewer laps.",
      alembist: "He took everything at the fixed hour, and we were slow with the limbs. Break them early, hit hard, and be done before he gathers it. Label: this batch spoils if you dawdle.",
      threadweaver: "He pulled every thread at once, and there was nothing left to hold. We'd been slow with the limbs. Next time, snip them early and finish him sooner.",
      marionettist: "The final curtain fell on the wrong side, with the company still on stage. We lingered over the limbs. Next time, break them quickly and be done before the house lights go. (Poor notices, and deserved.)",
      cleric: "He took everything, and not one of us got up. We had been too slow with the limbs. The fault was mine for being gentle. Forgive me, my friends. Sooner, next time.",
      grovekeeper: "He took the whole grove back, and us with it, like a late frost. We were slow with the limbs. Next time we prune quickly and finish early.",
      chronomancer: "Nothing Left comes at the same hour however many times we undo it. We were slow with the limbs. Next time, which was last time, we break them faster, and finish early.",
      tidecaller: "He took everything, like the tide going out for good. We had been slow with the limbs. Next time we break them sooner, and the fight is shorter for it.",
      lanternkeeper: "Every light went out at once. We ran out of oil: too slow with the limbs. Next time, break them early and finish while the lamps are still warm.",
      kenseier: "Nothing Left falls on the clock, not on a count. We were slow with the limbs. Next time they are down before it falls.",
      houndmaster: "Whole grove went, and us with it. Too slow with the limbs. Should've broken them quick and been finished first.",
    } },
    'Twin Seed': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "On the hard nights, two seeds at once, joined by a cord. Burst far apart they open two small holes; too close, and they open one giant hole between them. Two of the line walked to opposite edges, as far apart as the room allows.",
      gloomsworn: "On the hard nights, two seeds joined by a cord. Burst close together, they make one huge hole between them. So the two carry them to opposite edges, as far apart as the room allows. I have seen the inside of a big hole.",
      thresher: "On the hard night, dear, two seeds, and a cord between them. Stand too near each other and they open one giant hole between you. So off the two go, to opposite edges, as far apart as the room allows. Do mind the cord.",
      bloodreaver: "Hard nights, two seeds tied together! Too close and they make a giant hole, a monstrous one. So the two carriers ran to opposite edges, far apart, and stepped out of the rings as the ground split. Big hole, bigger grin, and none of us in it.",
      runeguard: "On the harder nights two seeds are joined by a cord and sit on two of us. Carry them to opposite edges, as far apart as the room allows, else they open one giant hole between them.",
      gorgemaw: "On the hard nights, two seeds, tied with string like a brace of sausages. Burst them close and they make one monstrous hole. We pulled them to opposite edges, as far apart as the table allows. A double helping, and twice as hollow.",
      oathbroker: "On the harder terms two seeds are bound by a cord. The clause: if the parties are close when they burst, one giant breach opens between them. The bearers therefore went to opposite edges, as far apart as the room allows.",
      ironpalm: "On hard nights, two seeds, joined. Close together, they speak as one, and the hole is huge. So the two went to opposite edges, as far apart as the room allows, and each opened its own. Two small words, not one great one.",
      ranger: "Hard nights: two seeds, joined by a cord. Burst them near each other, one giant hole between. Sent them to opposite edges, as far apart as possible. Far apart, by a mile.",
      shadowblade: "On the hard nights the mark ties two seeds together. Too close, and they open one giant hole between them, which is greedy. The two went to opposite edges, as far apart as the room allows, and each stepped out in time.",
      pyromancer: "Hard nights: two seeds on a cord, and if they burst close together they make a giant hole, a very rude one. The two ran to opposite edges, as far apart as they could get, and stepped out of their rings.",
      thermist: "Two seeds on one cord. Close together they open a single giant hole; far apart, two small ones. The carriers ran for opposite edges, the cord pulling taut between them, and stepped out as the ground split. I felt the tension from across the room.",
      driftblade: "On the hard nights there are two seeds joined by a cord, and if they burst close together they open one giant hole, so the two ran out through everyone to opposite edges, as far apart as the room allows, and stepped out as the ground split.",
      alembist: "Two seeds on one cord. Close together they open one giant hole; at opposite edges, two small ones. So the carriers went as far apart as the room allows and stepped out as the ground split. Do not share a sample.",
      threadweaver: "Hard nights, two seeds tied on a cord. Too close and the thread bunches into one giant hole. So the two drew it taut, one to each edge, as far apart as the room allows, and stepped out as the ground split.",
      marionettist: "On the hard nights, a pair of seeds joined by a cord. Too close and they open one enormous trapdoor between them. The two players exited to opposite wings, as far apart as the stage allows. (Exeunt, severally.)",
      cleric: "On the hard nights two seeds are bound by a cord. If their bearers stand close, one giant hole opens between them. So the two of them went to opposite edges, as far apart as the room allows, and stepped out in time.",
      grovekeeper: "On hard nights two seeds in one furrow, tied by a cord. Sown too close, they'd make one great hole between them. So my two walked to opposite edges, as far apart as the room allows, and stepped out as the ground split. Sow wide.",
      chronomancer: "On the hard nights the two seeds are tied together, and burst on the same beat. Too close and the hole is giant. So the two went to opposite edges, as far apart as the room allows. I said so earlier, or I will.",
      tidecaller: "Hard nights bring two seeds on one cord, like a pair of buoys. Too close, and they open one giant hole between them, a whirlpool. The two went to opposite shores, as far apart as the room allows.",
      lanternkeeper: "On the hard nights two seeds are tied together. If they burst close, one giant hole opens between. I walked with one, another with the other, to opposite edges, as far apart as the room allows. Two lights, two edges.",
      kenseier: "Two seeds on a cord. Both burst on the same beat, and if they are close, the hole between them is giant. The bearers went to opposite edges, as far apart as the room allows, and stepped out on the set.",
      houndmaster: "Hard nights, two seeds tied on a cord, like two dogs on one lead. Close together and they make a giant hole. Sent them to opposite edges, as far apart as the room goes.",
    } },
    'Hungering Dark': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "On the hard nights the ground around every hole leans in and drags whoever stands on it to the lip, with a hard blow. The ring is painted round each hole. The line kept outside every ring, and I kept the Oak clear of them.",
      gloomsworn: "On the hard nights the holes breathe in, and the ground round each drags whoever stands on it to the rim, hard. The ring is painted. We kept outside every one, and no one was left on a lip. I would rather not be dragged.",
      thresher: "On the hard night, dear, the holes breathe in, and the ground in a ring round each one drags whoever stands on it to the edge, and hits them besides. The rings are painted, so stay outside them all.",
      bloodreaver: "Hard nights, and the holes breathe in! The ground in a ring round each drags you to the lip and hits you hard on the way. The rings are painted, so I stayed outside them. No scar from that one, and I'm almost sorry.",
      runeguard: "On the harder nights the ground round each hole draws in, hits hard, and leaves you at the lip. A ring is painted round every hole, and the sensible course is to stand outside every ring.",
      gorgemaw: "On the hard nights each hole breathes in and the ground about it drags a guest to the lip and thumps him on the way. The rings are painted round each hole. We ate elsewhere.",
      oathbroker: "On the harder terms each hole draws the ground round it inward: a heavy penalty, and the party left at the lip. The ring is painted round every hole. We remained outside all of them.",
      ironpalm: "Hard nights, the holes inhale. The earth around each pulls toward its edge and strikes heavily on the way. The circles are marked on the floor. We stayed beyond every one of them, and the pull found no one.",
      ranger: "Hard nights: holes breathe in. Ground round each drags you to the lip, hard hit. Rings are painted. Stay outside all of them.",
      shadowblade: "On the hard nights the holes breathe in and the ground round each one drags whoever stands on it to the lip, with a hard blow. The rings are painted. I stayed outside every one.",
      pyromancer: "Hard nights: every hole breathes in, and the ground round it drags you to the lip with a wallop! The rings are painted, so I stayed outside every one and set fire to the air instead.",
      thermist: "The holes breathe in, and the ground in a ring round each one drags you to the lip with a hard blow. The rings are painted. Stay outside every one. I felt the pull in my teeth and kept my distance.",
      driftblade: "On the hard nights the holes breathe in and the ground round each one drags whoever is on it to the lip with a hard blow, so I watched the painted rings and ran between them, never in one, and wide of every edge.",
      alembist: "The ground round each hole is drawn inward, a hard blow, and the patient ends on the lip. The rings are painted, so stay outside every one. I would rather not be the specimen on the edge.",
      threadweaver: "On the hard nights the holes draw in like a purse-string, and the ground round each one drags whoever stands on it to the lip with a hard blow. The rings are painted. I kept every stitch outside them.",
      marionettist: "On the hard nights the trapdoors breathe in, and the boards round each one drag a player to the lip with a thump. The rings are painted on the stage. We kept to the wings. (Exeunt, outside the rings.)",
      cleric: "On the hard nights the ground round every hole leans in and drags whoever stands on it to the lip, with a hard blow. The rings are painted, so I asked everyone to stay outside them all.",
      grovekeeper: "On hard nights the ground round every hole draws in like roots to a drain, and drags whoever stands on it to the lip with a hard blow. The rings are painted. Mine stayed outside them.",
      chronomancer: "On the hard nights the holes breathe in just after the rings glow, and the ground drags whoever is on it to the lip, hard. The rings are painted. We stood outside them, the only sensible place, and I mean that retroactively.",
      tidecaller: "On the hard nights each hole draws in like a whirlpool: the ground round it drags whoever stands there to the lip with a hard blow. The rings are painted. We kept to still water.",
      lanternkeeper: "On the hard nights the ground round each hole draws in and drags whoever is on it to the lip, hard. The rings are painted, so I lit the clear way between them.",
      kenseier: "On the hard nights each hole draws a long breath and the ground round it hauls whoever stands there to the lip, hard. The rings are painted before the count. I read them, and we stood outside.",
      houndmaster: "Hole breathes in, and the ground round it hauls you to the lip with a wallop. Rings are painted, easy to see. Whistled the dogs back and stayed outside the lot.",
    } },
    'Deeper Roots': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "On the hard nights he rises with older limbs among the four: a wyrm's wing, a frost giant's fist, an archmage's rune, a hydra's head. Four are drawn from eight each time, and a different four each fight. We read which four before we chose the order.",
      gloomsworn: "On the hard nights he grows his four limbs from eight, the old ones and older still: a wyrm's wing, a frost fist, an archmage's rune, a hydra's head join the cleaver, the roots, the storm and the lantern. Look before you break. I knew some of them dead.",
      thresher: "On the hard night, dear, he draws his four limbs from eight: the old four, and a wyrm's wing, a frost giant's fist, an archmage's rune, a hydra's head. Different ones every time, so look first. Each has its own bar.",
      bloodreaver: "Hard nights, four limbs picked from eight! The old four, and the wyrm's wing, the frost giant's fist, the archmage's rune, the hydra's head, all from fights I loved. Different every pull. Look first, then break them in whatever order.",
      runeguard: "On the harder nights he draws four limbs from eight: the old four, and the wyrm's wing, the frost giant's fist, the archmage's rune, the hydra's head. The omen is which four grow, which differs every pull. Read them first, then choose the order.",
      gorgemaw: "On the hard nights my host serves four limbs from a menu of eight, with the wyrm's wing, the frost giant's fist, the archmage's rune and the hydra's head among them. Different every time. Read the menu, then break them in the order you fancy.",
      oathbroker: "On the harder terms, four limbs are drawn from eight, adding the wyrm's wing, the frost giant's fist, the archmage's rune and the hydra's head to the earlier four. The draw differs at each sitting. Read it first, then settle in the order preferred.",
      ironpalm: "On hard nights he spoke with four voices drawn from eight: the old four, and the wyrm's wing, the frost giant's fist, the archmage's rune, the hydra's head. The four change each time. We listened first, then chose the order.",
      ranger: "Hard nights: four limbs drawn from eight. The old four plus wyrm wing, frost fist, archmage rune, hydra head. Different four every pull. Read them first, then pick.",
      shadowblade: "On the hard nights the mark grows four limbs, drawn from eight: the old ones, and a wyrm's wing, a frost giant's fist, an archmage's rune, a hydra's head. A different four every time, so look before choosing.",
      pyromancer: "Hard nights: he grows four limbs from EIGHT, including a wyrm's wing, a frost giant's fist, an archmage's rune and a hydra's head! A different four every pull, which is rude of him, so look first. Then choose the order.",
      thermist: "Four limbs drawn from eight: the old four, plus a wyrm's wing, a frost fist, an archmage's rune and a hydra's head. Different every pull, so read the four before touching one. I wanted to break the worst at once. Looking first is cheaper.",
      driftblade: "On the hard nights he grows four limbs drawn from eight, with a wyrm's wing and a frost giant's fist and an archmage's rune and a hydra's head among them, a different four every time, so I looked first and cut through them in whatever order suited.",
      alembist: "Specimen: second form, hard pattern. Four limbs drawn from eight: the old four, plus wyrm wing, frost fist, archmage rune and hydra head. The draw changes every pull. Note: read the four before the first break. Label: keep the notes.",
      threadweaver: "On the hard nights he's sewn together from eight limbs and wears four: the old four, and the wyrm's wing, the frost giant's fist, the archmage's rune, the hydra's head. A different four every time. Check the seams first.",
      marionettist: "On the hard nights the wardrobe holds eight costumes and he wears four: the old four, and a wyrm's wing, a frost giant's fist, an archmage's rune and a hydra's head. A different four every night. (Check the programme.)",
      cleric: "On the hard nights he grows four limbs drawn from eight, and older monsters have joined the lot: a wyrm, a frost giant, an archmage, a hydra. Never the same four twice. We looked first, then chose the order.",
      grovekeeper: "On hard nights he grafts four limbs from a stock of eight. Beside the old four, a wyrm's wing, a frost giant's fist, an archmage's rune, a hydra's head. A different crop every pull. Look before you prune.",
      chronomancer: "On the hard nights the limbs are drawn four from eight, so no pull is the last pull. The wyrm's wing, frost giant's fist, archmage's rune and hydra's head join the old four. Read them before the first break. I had read them before the draw was made.",
      tidecaller: "On the hard nights he draws four limbs from eight, as a net draws fish: the old four, and a wyrm's wing, a frost giant's fist, an archmage's rune, a hydra's head. A different catch every pull. Read it first.",
      lanternkeeper: "He wears four limbs out of eight on the hard nights, and a different four each pull: the old ones, or a wyrm's wing, a frost giant's fist, an archmage's rune, a hydra's head. I held my lamp up to each before anyone swung.",
      kenseier: "On the hard nights he grows four limbs drawn from eight: the old four, and a wyrm's wing, a frost giant's fist, an archmage's rune and a hydra's head. Some of these I have crossed blades with. Read the four first.",
      houndmaster: "Hard nights, the big lad wears four limbs out of eight. The old ones, or a wyrm's wing, a frost giant's fist, an archmage's rune, a hydra's head, never the same four twice. Had a good look before we broke anything.",
    } },
    'Wyrm Wing': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "The wyrm's wing swept one whole half of the room, the half on the wing's side of him. The painted half told me which. The line went to the other half, and I held the Oak still for it.",
      gloomsworn: "The wyrm's wing swept a whole half of the room, the side the wing grows on. The painted half is plain enough. We crossed to the other half. A wyrm and a tree is a tiring combination.",
      thresher: "The wyrm's wing, dear, sweeps a whole half of the room, the half the wing hangs on, painted so you can see. Cross to the other half, and hurry. Such a big wing.",
      bloodreaver: "The wyrm's old wing swept half the room, the half it hangs on! The painted half was huge. We crossed to the other half. I missed the wyrm's fire, the real thing. I wanted the burn and kept my skin.",
      runeguard: "The wing stirs on its side, and half of the room is painted. The whole party crosses to the other half before it sweeps. The wyrm's wing has its own entry in the margin.",
      gorgemaw: "The wyrm's wing, served with half the room, the half the wing hangs on, painted. We crossed to the other side. Scorched, and tough as a cheap cut.",
      oathbroker: "The wyrm's instrument is a wing, sweeping one half of the room, the half on its side, painted in advance. All parties removed to the opposite half.",
      ironpalm: "The wyrm spoke through a wing: half the room, the half it hangs on, painted. We crossed to the other half and listened from there. It struck empty floor.",
      ranger: "Wyrm's wing sweeps half the room, painted, on the wing's side. Crossed to the other half. Cross early.",
      shadowblade: "The wyrm's wing sweeps half the room, the half it hangs on, painted. I crossed to the other half early, and was waiting when it passed.",
      pyromancer: "The wyrm's wing! The old fire, half the room's worth, the half the wing hangs on, painted bright. We crossed to the other half, and I admired it from there.",
      thermist: "The wyrm's wing sweeps half the room, the half it hangs on, painted bright. Cross to the other half before it falls. The heat off it was almost homely, and I crossed early all the same.",
      driftblade: "The wyrm's wing swept half the room, the half it hangs on, painted clear, so I ran through the middle and out into the other half and watched it sweep past behind me.",
      alembist: "The wing sweeps the half of the room it hangs on, and paints it first. Cross to the other half before it falls; early beats late. It scorches.",
      threadweaver: "The wyrm's wing swept half the room, the side it hangs on, painted. My stitches crossed to the other half together, so nobody was left on the hem.",
      marionettist: "The wyrm's wing sweeps half the stage, the half it hangs on, chalked clear. The whole company crossed to the other half, quickly. (Stage left! Stage left!)",
      cleric: "The wyrm's wing swept half the room, the half it hangs on, painted clear. I gathered everyone and crossed to the other half before it fell. A kindness, that paint.",
      grovekeeper: "The wyrm's wing swept half the room like a hot wind, the half it hangs on, painted clear. We moved to the other half before the wind came. Early, like a hand ahead of the weather.",
      chronomancer: "The wing sweeps half the room just after the painting finishes. We crossed to the other half as the paint went down. I was there already, by my reckoning.",
      tidecaller: "The wyrm's wing swept half the room like a wave breaking on one side, painted clear. We crossed to the other half, patient and early.",
      lanternkeeper: "The wyrm's wing swept half the room, the side it hangs on, painted clear. I lit the other half and we stood there before it fell. All four of us, the right half.",
      kenseier: "The wyrm's wing sweeps half the room with a long count, the half it hangs on. A blade cannot turn a whole half. We crossed early to the other half.",
      houndmaster: "Wyrm's wing sweeps half the room, the half it hangs on, painted. Crossed over to the other half, dogs too.",
    } },
    'Frost Fist': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "The frost giant's fist chose the one standing farthest from the Oak. A great ring followed that one, then set. They stepped out as it set, and the rest kept off it. The ice it left slowed anyone it touched, so we kept off that too.",
      gloomsworn: "The frost giant's fist picked whoever stood farthest from the Oak. A great ring followed that one, then set. They stepped out of it. Ice stayed behind and slowed whoever stood in it, so we kept off it too. Cold I can do. Slow I cannot.",
      thresher: "The frost giant's fist, dear, goes to whoever stands farthest from him, a great ring that follows them, then sets. Step out of it, everyone else keep clear, and mind the ice it leaves, which slows.",
      bloodreaver: "The frost fist picked whoever stood farthest from the big one! A huge ring followed that one, then set. They stepped out, and the rest kept clear. The ice left behind slows anyone who stands in it, so we stayed off. A cold one.",
      runeguard: "A ring follows the one who stands farthest from him, then sets. That one steps out as it sets, the rest keep clear, and none of us stand in the ice that is left, for it slows.",
      gorgemaw: "The frost giant's fist, served cold to whoever sat farthest from the host. A huge ring followed that guest, then set. They stepped out, we kept clear, and nobody sat in the ice it left, which slows. Frozen, and flavourless.",
      oathbroker: "The frost giant's instrument is a fist, directed at the party standing farthest from the counterparty. A great ring follows them, then sets. They vacate it; the others stay clear; the ice left behind, which slows, is avoided.",
      ironpalm: "The frost fist came for whoever stood farthest from the Oak. A great ring followed them, then settled. They stepped out of it, and we kept off. The ice stayed, and slowed. We went around.",
      ranger: "Frost fist goes to whoever's farthest from him. Big ring follows, then sets. Stepped out. Rest kept clear. Ice left behind slows, stayed off it.",
      shadowblade: "The frost giant's fist chooses whoever stands farthest from the mark, which is rarely me. A great ring follows them, then sets. They stepped out; we kept clear; nobody stood in the ice it left.",
      pyromancer: "The frost giant's fist! It went for whoever stood farthest from him, a huge ring that followed them and then set. They stepped out, we kept clear, and I stayed well off the ice it left, which slows.",
      thermist: "The frost fist goes to whoever stands farthest from the Oak: a ring follows them, then sets. They step out as it sets, the rest keep clear, and everyone stays off the ice it leaves, which slows. Cold is my country. Slowness is not.",
      driftblade: "The frost giant's fist went for whoever was farthest from the Oak, a great ring that followed them and set, so they ran out of it through the others and everyone kept off the ice it left, which slows, and I especially.",
      alembist: "A huge ring follows whoever is farthest from the Oak, then sets. They step out, the rest keep clear, and nobody stands in the ice it leaves, which slows. Thawing takes a while, so do not test it.",
      threadweaver: "The frost fist chose whoever stood farthest from the Oak, a huge ring that followed them. They stepped out as it set, and my stitches kept clear. The ice it left slows, so we cut round it.",
      marionettist: "The frost giant's fist: a great ring following the player farthest from the star. They stepped out as it set, the company kept clear, and nobody stood in the ice, which slows. (Exit, cold. Applause, stiff.)",
      cleric: "Whoever stood farthest from the old tree had a great ring following them, then setting. They stepped out as it set, the rest of us kept clear, and I scolded anyone drifting onto the ice it left, which slows.",
      grovekeeper: "A late frost came down in a ring on whoever stood farthest from the old oak, following them, then setting hard. They stepped out as it set, my lot stayed clear, and nobody wandered onto the ice, which slows.",
      chronomancer: "The fist follows whoever is farthest, then sets a moment later. They stepped out on the count, the rest kept clear, and the ice it left, which slows, was left alone. I said so early; I will say so again.",
      tidecaller: "The frost giant's fist chose whoever stood farthest from the Oak, a huge ring, following, then setting, like ice forming on a pool. They stepped out; the rest kept clear; the ice left behind slows, so we stayed off.",
      lanternkeeper: "The frost giant's fist chose whoever stood farthest from the Oak. A huge ring followed them, then set. I lit their way out of it and we all kept clear. The ice it leaves slows anyone in it. We stayed on the warm side.",
      kenseier: "The frost giant's fist goes to whoever stands farthest, and the ring follows, then sets on a count. They stepped out; we kept clear. The ice left behind slows, and a slow man misses his beat.",
      houndmaster: "Frost fist went for whoever stood farthest from the old bull. Big ring followed him, then set. Stepped out. Dogs stayed off the ice, it slows.",
    } },
    'Archmage Rune': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "A great rune circle opened at the Oak's feet, painted. We stood out of it until it burst, then stepped inside before the ring beyond it fell. Out, then in.",
      gloomsworn: "The archmage's rune opened at his feet: a circle first, then the ring outside it. Out of the circle until it burst, then into the middle before the ring. I did not expect to meet a wizard in a tree.",
      thresher: "The archmage's rune, dear, a great circle at his feet that bursts first, and then a ring outside it. So out of the circle, and when it bursts, in, before the ring falls. Out, then in.",
      bloodreaver: "The archmage's glowing rune at the big one's feet! A circle that bursts first, then the ring outside it. Out until the circle goes, then in before the ring! Out, then in. A good one, if you're quick.",
      runeguard: "A rune circle is drawn at his feet, and a ring beyond it. Stand outside the circle until it bursts, then step in before the ring does. Out, then in.",
      gorgemaw: "The archmage's rune, a circle at my host's feet and then a ring outside it, like a plate and a rim. Out of the plate until it bursts, then in before the rim. A course in two parts. Overcooked.",
      oathbroker: "The archmage's rune: a circle is entered at his feet, then the ring outside it. The clause: stay outside the circle until it discharges, then step inside before the ring does. Out, then in.",
      ironpalm: "The archmage's rune opened at his feet. A circle, then the ring outside it. A question, then an answer. We stood out until the circle burst, then stepped in before the ring. Out, then in.",
      ranger: "Rune circle at his feet, then the ring beyond it. Out until the circle bursts. In before the ring. Out, then in.",
      shadowblade: "The archmage's rune: a circle at the mark's feet, then the ring outside it. Out until the circle burst, then in at once, where nobody would think to look.",
      pyromancer: "The archmage's rune! A glowing circle at his feet, then a ring outside it! I stood outside until the circle burst, then dashed inside before the ring fell. Out, then in. A beautiful bit of choreography.",
      thermist: "A rune circle at his feet, then a ring outside it. Out of the circle until it bursts, then in like a flare before the ring falls. Out, then in. The ground inside was warm, which I took for a kindness.",
      driftblade: "The archmage's rune opened a circle at his feet and then a ring outside it, so I ran around outside the circle until it burst and then through into the middle before the ring fell, out and then in, without once touching down.",
      alembist: "The rune is a circle at his feet and a ring outside it. Stay outside the circle until it bursts, then go in before the ring falls; the middle is safe the second time. Out, then in, like pouring and stoppering.",
      threadweaver: "The archmage's rune: a circle at his feet, then the ring outside it. My stitches stood outside the circle until it burst, then drew in tight before the ring fell. Out, then in. A neat bit of cutting.",
      marionettist: "The archmage's rune: a spotlight at his feet, then the ring outside it. The company stood outside the spot until it blazed, then crossed in before the ring. (Out, then in. Never both.)",
      cleric: "A wide circle opened at the oak's feet, the archmage's rune, and a wider ring beyond it. I kept everyone back until the circle had burst, then walked them into the middle before the ring could fall. Out first, in after.",
      grovekeeper: "The archmage's rune, a circle at his feet and then a ring outside it, like a fairy ring. We stood out until the circle burst, then stepped in before the ring did. Out, then in.",
      chronomancer: "The rune's circle bursts first, and a beat later the ring outside it. We waited outside the circle, then stepped in on the beat. Out, then in. I will have said it earlier.",
      tidecaller: "The archmage's rune: a circle at his feet bursting, and a wave of ring outside it a few swells later. We stood out until the circle burst, then stepped in before the ring. Out, then in.",
      lanternkeeper: "The archmage's rune: a circle at his feet, and then a ring outside it. We stood outside the circle until it burst, then I lit the middle and we stepped in before the ring fell. Out, then in.",
      kenseier: "The archmage's rune: two strokes, a circle and then the ring outside it. The first has a count; the second follows soon after. We stood out until the circle burst, then stepped in. Out, then in.",
      houndmaster: "Archmage's rune at the old bull's feet: a circle first, then a ring outside it. Stood out till the circle went, then in before the ring, dogs too. Out, then in.",
    } },
    'Hydra Head': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "The hydra's head took aim along a painted line from the limb to whoever stood farthest from it. That one stepped aside, and the line stayed on empty floor. The others stayed out of it.",
      gloomsworn: "The hydra's head painted a line from the limb to whoever stood farthest away. They stepped aside, and it fell on empty floor. The rest of us kept off the line. I have met a hydra; this one is less sociable.",
      thresher: "The hydra's head, dear, paints a line from the limb to whoever stands farthest from it. Step aside, and everyone keep off the line. Such a long reach.",
      bloodreaver: "The hydra's head breathed a line at whoever stood farthest from it! It was painted first, so they stepped aside, and the rest kept off the line. I did consider standing in it. Barely a scratch, avoided.",
      runeguard: "A line is painted from the limb to the one who stands farthest from it. That one steps aside, and the rest keep off the line. The hydra's head kept to its page.",
      gorgemaw: "The hydra's head, which breathes along a painted line at whoever sits farthest from it. That guest stepped aside, and the rest kept off the line. Wet, and a little reptilian.",
      oathbroker: "The hydra's head is directed along a painted line at the party farthest from it. That party stepped aside; the others kept off the line.",
      ironpalm: "A line was drawn on the floor from the limb toward the one farthest from it. That one moved a single step and the hydra's breath found nothing. The rest of us stayed clear of the line, and said nothing.",
      ranger: "Hydra head. Line painted to whoever's farthest. They stepped aside. Rest stayed off the line.",
      shadowblade: "The hydra's head aimed a painted line at whoever was farthest from it. They stepped aside, the rest of us stood off the line, and it hit nothing. Rude of it to try.",
      pyromancer: "The hydra's head! A line painted to whoever stood farthest from it, and then a breath down it. They stepped aside; we kept off the line. A far better breath than mine.",
      thermist: "The hydra's head paints a line to whoever stands farthest from it. They step aside; the rest keep off the line. The breath that followed was warm. Not warm enough to interest me.",
      driftblade: "The hydra's head painted a line to whoever stood farthest from it, so they stepped aside and I stayed off the line and ran round it, through the gap behind it, and the breath went past and hit only floor.",
      alembist: "The head paints its line before the breath, to whoever stands farthest from it. That one steps aside, and everyone else keeps off the line. Labelled it reptile and left it at that.",
      threadweaver: "The hydra's head painted a line to whoever stood farthest from it, long and straight as a seam. They stepped aside, and the rest of us kept off the line.",
      marionettist: "The hydra's head: a line painted to whoever stood farthest from it. They stepped aside, and the rest of the company kept off the line. (The spotlight missed. Booed.)",
      cleric: "A long line was painted across the floor toward the one standing farthest from the hydra's head, and I watched that friend step calmly aside while the rest of us stayed off the line. No breath of it touched anyone. I counted it a quiet blessing.",
      grovekeeper: "The hydra's head painted a line to whoever stood farthest from it, like a creeper along a wall. They stepped aside; the rest of us kept off the line.",
      chronomancer: "The line is painted first, and the breath follows along it. Whoever stood farthest stepped aside, and the rest kept off. I was already out of the way, give or take a bell.",
      tidecaller: "The hydra's head painted a line to whoever stood farthest from it, like a current finding a swimmer. They stepped aside, and the rest kept off the line.",
      lanternkeeper: "A painted line ran from the head out to the one standing farthest away. I lit a lantern over them, they moved clear, and the blow landed on bare stone while the rest of us stayed out of its path.",
      kenseier: "The hydra's head: a breath along a painted line, with a count. It goes to whoever stands farthest. They stepped aside, and the rest kept off the line.",
      houndmaster: "Hydra head painted a line to whoever stood farthest from it. Stepped aside. Whistled the dogs back from the line, and kept clear myself.",
    } },
    'Last Gasp': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "On the hard nights a broken limb gives one last blow, from where it fell, the same as before. I broke them, then held the ground and waited for the last one. It came. We moved.",
      gloomsworn: "On the hard nights a broken limb does not stay broken. Its blow comes once more, from where it fell. I have been on the receiving end of last breaths. We moved as before.",
      thresher: "On the hard night, dear, a limb that is broken still gets its last say: its move comes once more from the spot where it fell. Do mind it, and don't cheer too soon.",
      bloodreaver: "On the hard nights a broken limb gets one more go, from where it fell! I like a limb with spirit. We dealt with it the same way as the first time, only more tired.",
      runeguard: "On the harder nights a broken limb fires once more from where it fell, to the same text as before. So the same reading serves, and we were ready for it.",
      gorgemaw: "On the hard nights, a limb struck from the menu is brought back once more, from where it fell, for one more course. The same dish, the same remedy. Reheated, and nothing new.",
      oathbroker: "On the harder terms, a limb broken is not discharged. Its obligation falls due once more from where it fell. The remedy is the same as the first time.",
      ironpalm: "On hard nights a broken limb speaks once more, from where it fell. The same word, the same answer. We did not forget.",
      ranger: "Hard nights: broken limb fires once more from where it fell. Same move, same answer. Don't relax when it drops.",
      shadowblade: "On the hard nights a broken limb fires one last time from where it fell, which is rude of a corpse. Same move as before, same answer.",
      pyromancer: "On the hard nights a broken limb fires once more from where it fell! I could hardly believe the encore. Same move, same answer. I should not cheer too soon.",
      thermist: "A broken limb fires once more from where it fell. Same move, same answer. I cheered at the break and went hot with embarrassment when it spoke again.",
      driftblade: "On the hard nights a broken limb fires once more from where it fell, the very same move, so I broke it and kept running and was already through its path when it came, and never stood still for the encore.",
      alembist: "A broken limb repeats its blow once from where it fell. The answer is the same as the first time, so do not relax after the break. A second dose, same measure.",
      threadweaver: "On the hard nights a broken limb gives one more go, from where it fell. The same move as before, so the same stitch in answer. A cut thread can still snap back, so keep your hands clear.",
      marionettist: "On the hard nights a broken limb takes a curtain call: its move fires once more, from where it fell. (Encore! The company knew the blocking.)",
      cleric: "On the hard nights a broken limb gives one last blow from where it fell. The same one as before, so the same answer. I do not like a goodbye that bites.",
      grovekeeper: "On the hard nights a cut limb still has one last season in it: its move comes again from where it fell. The same move, the same answer. Don't relax at the pruning.",
      chronomancer: "On the hard nights the broken limb fires once more from where it fell, soon after it breaks. Same move, same answer. I knew it was coming, which helped nobody.",
      tidecaller: "On the hard nights a broken limb gives one more swell from where it fell. The same move as before, the same answer. The wave always breaks twice.",
      lanternkeeper: "On the hard nights a broken limb fires once more from where it fell. The same move as before, so I lit the same spots as before. A last flicker, then dark.",
      kenseier: "On the hard nights a broken limb gives one last stroke from where it fell. The same stroke, the same count, the same answer. A fallen blade still has an edge.",
      houndmaster: "Hard nights, broken limb fires once more from where it fell. Same move, same answer. Don't call off the dogs till it's done.",
    } },
    'Greenseed': { hard: false, extreme: true, learned: {
      bastion: "Two of the line carried seeds, one green, one violet. Mine was the green: I walked it to the lip of the nearest hole and held there, and the pit knitted shut. The violet bearer went the other way, to the rim of the room.",
      gloomsworn: "A green sign over one of us, violet over another. The green is kind, which I did not expect of this place. Stand it against a pit's lip and the pit is filled for good. Violet still wants a grave of its own, so it goes far off.",
      thresher: "A small green seed, dear, over one head, and a violet over another. Green wants a hole to mend, so off it goes to the lip of one, and the hole is gone for good. Violet is the greedy one: out to the far edge with it.",
      bloodreaver: "Green seed on one head, violet on another! The green one doesn't bite. I marched it up to a hole and the hole healed over, scar and all. The violet one went off to dig a new pit at the edge, far from the healed one.",
      runeguard: "The two signs differ: green over one head, violet over another. The green is the remedy, harmless, and it must lie against a hole's rim to close that hole for ever. The violet is the old appetite, carried to the room's edge, apart from the repair.",
      gorgemaw: "Two garnishes arrived, green and violet. The green is harmless, a palate cleanser: set it against a hole and the hole is filled back in. The violet is the old dish, ravenous as ever, and went to the far rim of the room.",
      oathbroker: "Two seeds are issued, green and violet. The green is without penalty; positioned against a hole, it discharges that hole permanently. The violet creates a breach as before, and its bearer places it at the room's edge, away from the discharged one.",
      ironpalm: "One seed green, one violet. The green was a gentle word: I set it beside a hole, and the hole closed. The violet still spoke hunger, so its bearer took it out to the edge and spoke it there.",
      ranger: "Green seed, violet seed. Green's harmless; get it to a hole's lip and the hole shuts. Violet digs, so the far edge for that one, well off the repair.",
      shadowblade: "A green seed over one of us, a violet over another. I slipped the green one up beside a hole while nobody was looking, and the hole simply stopped existing. The violet was sent to dig somewhere dull, out at the edge.",
      pyromancer: "Two seeds! Green on one head, violet on another, and the green is a gift: it fills a hole in, for good, if its ring lands against the edge! The violet still digs, so we sent it far out where its hole can't hurt.",
      thermist: "Green is cool, violet hot. The green ring harms nobody; I walked it to a hole's edge and the pit closed over. The violet dug where it always digs, out at the rim, and I made sure the two stayed far apart.",
      driftblade: "Two seeds, green and violet, so I ran the green round the edge of a hole and it sealed it for good, and the violet ran the other way and dug a pit nobody would miss, out where the floor ends.",
      alembist: "Sample one, green: harmless, and it seals a hole when its ring touches the lip. Sample two, violet: digs as before. Keep the two apart, one to the pit, the other to the far edge. Label both before use.",
      threadweaver: "Green seed on one of my stitches, violet on another. The green mends: walk it up to a hole's lip and the tear is darned shut. The violet is the moth, and it went to the far edge to chew somewhere cheap.",
      marionettist: "Two seeds were cast, green and violet. Green is the repair crew: it takes its mark at the lip of a trapdoor and the trapdoor is boarded over. Violet cuts a new one, so it plays its scene in the far wings.",
      cleric: "A green seed fell on one friend and a violet on another. The green wounds no one, and where it touches a hole's lip the wound in the floor closes. I asked the violet bearer to go far off, to the edge, away from it.",
      grovekeeper: "A green seed, a violet seed. The green is a graft, the first kind thing this tree has done: ring against a hole's lip and the soil closes over it. The violet goes to the far edge to be a hole there.",
      chronomancer: "Green over one head, violet over another. The green undoes a hole, which pleases me: its ring touches the lip, and the pit is as it never was. The violet digs a moment later, far off at the edge, as it should.",
      tidecaller: "Two seeds, green and violet. The green is the incoming tide: it harms no one, and against a pit's lip it fills it up. The violet is the undertow, and it was sent far out to the edge to pull at nothing.",
      lanternkeeper: "Green over one of us, violet over another. I lit a lamp at a hole's lip, and the green carrier stood by it; the hole closed. Violet went to the far edge with a lamp of its own, one light each.",
      kenseier: "Green and violet. The green has no blow in it: bring its ring to a hole's lip and the hole closes on the count. The violet still digs, so it goes far to the edge, a long way from the repair.",
      houndmaster: "Seed on two of us, green and violet. Green does no harm: walked it up to a hole and the hole filled in for good. Violet still digs, so out to the far edge with it. Dogs stayed well back from both.",
    } },
    'Take Root': { hard: false, extreme: true, learned: {
      bastion: "Rings opened, the petals painted before the pull. The one in the gap is the place for the line: it pins you until the first snap and the petals miss you. Without a ring, the haul takes you to him, and a hole on the road is a fall.",
      gloomsworn: "Three rings, petals painted early. Held in a gap is a fine place to be held; held under a petal is not. Outside the rings the haul no longer stops at a hole, and falling is a heavy blow. I took the gap ring. I am good at standing.",
      thresher: "Three blue rings, dear, and the petals laid out already. Pick the ring that sits between two petals and let the roots hold you there past the first snap. If you stay out of the rings, mind there is no hole between you and him.",
      bloodreaver: "Three rings and the petals already painted! I picked the one in the gap and let the roots clamp me there while the first snap missed me. Those out in the open got dragged, and a hole on the road meant a drop.",
      runeguard: "The bar writes three rings upon the floor beside the painted petals: one lies in a gap, two beneath petals. The sound course is the gap ring. Outside them the haul no longer halts at a hole, so a pit on the road means a fall.",
      gorgemaw: "Three rings like three place settings, and the petals already carved. Only one setting sits in a gap, so I took it and stayed seated through the first snap. The unseated are hauled to my host, pits and all.",
      oathbroker: "Three rings are drawn, and the petals with them. One ring lies in a gap, two under petals; a party within a ring is held to the first snap. Parties outside are hauled to him, and a hole on the way is forfeit.",
      ironpalm: "Three rings, petals painted before the pull. I stood in the ring in the gap. It held me, and the first snap missed. The ones outside were dragged to him, and anyone crossing a hole fell.",
      ranger: "Petals already down, rings beside them. One ring in a gap; the others under petals. Stand in the gap one. Outside, the haul drags you, and a hole in the path drops you.",
      shadowblade: "Three rings, petals painted early, which spoils the surprise. I chose the ring in the gap and let it hold me through the first snap. Anyone outside gets dragged to him, and any hole in between is a fall.",
      pyromancer: "Three blue rings, petals already glowing! I planted myself in the gap ring and was held there, nicely still, while the first snap missed me. In the open you're hauled to him, and a hole on the road is a drop.",
      thermist: "The petals painted already, rings among them. The gap ring holds you safe through the first snap; a ring under a petal holds you in the red. I took the cool one and stayed put. Out in the open, a hole on the road is a fall.",
      driftblade: "Three rings, petals painted early, so I picked the ring sitting in a gap and was held in the clear, and the ones outside were dragged to him, hole or no hole, and any hole they crossed took them.",
      alembist: "Three rings, petals already painted. Prescription: the gap ring, held until the first snap. Contraindicated: a ring under a petal, or standing outside with a hole between you and him; the haul ends in the hole.",
      threadweaver: "Rings on the floor, the petals painted beside them. I took the one in the gap and was held fast till the first snap. Any stitch left out in the open was hauled to him on a taut thread, and a hole on the road meant a fall.",
      marionettist: "Three rings chalked on the boards, the petals already painted. I held my mark in the gap ring, fixed through the first snap. Players outside were hauled downstage, and a trapdoor on the way was a fall.",
      cleric: "Three blue rings, the petals painted early. I kept to the ring in the gap, held fast till the first snap, and asked the others to do likewise, or at least to keep a hole off their road.",
      grovekeeper: "Three rings opened like rooting circles, petals painted already. One lies in a gap, so I took root there until the first snap. Those outside were drawn to him, and a hole on the road swallowed them.",
      chronomancer: "Three rings, the petals painted early, the haul a moment later. The ring in the gap holds you safely past the first snap. Outside, a hole in the way is no longer a stop but a fall. I chose the gap, as I will have.",
      tidecaller: "Rings like eddies on the floor, the petals painted ahead of the pull. The eddy between two petals holds you still and dry past the first snap. Out in the open, the drag no longer minds a hole's lip, and the hole is a fall.",
      lanternkeeper: "Petals painted early, rings among them. I hung a lamp over the gap ring and stood under it, held until the first snap. Without a ring you are hauled to him, so keep any hole off your road.",
      kenseier: "A long count before the haul, and rings on the floor beneath painted petals. I chose the one between two petals, and the roots held me still while the first stroke passed. A man in the open is hauled in, and a hole on the way takes him.",
      houndmaster: "Rings down, petals painted already. Took the one in the gap, held till the first snap, dogs by me. Anyone out in the open gets hauled to the big lad, and a hole on the road drops you.",
    } },
    'Grafting': { hard: false, extreme: true, learned: {
      bastion: "When a limb broke, its blow went to the next limb round his side, and that one did both on its turn. A limb that already carried one hands the lot on. So I watched which limb inherited, and kept the Oak still for two bars in a row.",
      gloomsworn: "A broken limb does not take its blow with it. The wood runs along to the next limb round his side, which now does both, one after the other. I know what it is to be carried on after the end. Break the heir soon, or be ready for two.",
      thresher: "Break a limb, dear, and its trick is handed to the next one round his side, which then does both. If it was carrying one already, it hands the lot along. Choose the order so the heir is not left to sing two songs.",
      bloodreaver: "A limb broke and its blow wasn't gone, it ran along the bark to the next limb, which did both in one go! Two for one. A limb with grafts hands the lot on. I broke the heir fast and enjoyed the other.",
      runeguard: "A short bar marks it: a broken limb's move passes to the next limb round his side, which does its own and then the other. One already carrying hands on all it holds. Read the heir before choosing the next limb to break.",
      gorgemaw: "A limb struck from the menu is not gone, it is served again by its neighbour, the next one round his side, as a second course after its own. A limb already carrying hands on the whole tray. Choose which plate you break with care.",
      oathbroker: "A broken limb's obligation is assigned to the next limb round his side, which then performs both in turn. A limb already bearing assigned moves transfers them all. The parties should know the heir before breaking.",
      ironpalm: "A limb broke, and its word was not gone. It was handed on to the limb beside it, which spoke both, one after the other, its own first. A limb already holding words passed them all along. I listened for the heir.",
      ranger: "Limb breaks, its move goes to the next limb round his side. That one does both, back to back. A limb carrying grafts hands them all on. Know the heir before you shoot the next one.",
      shadowblade: "A broken limb leaves its trick to the next limb round his side, which does both in a row. One already carrying hands on all of it. I looked at the heir before I broke the next limb, the way one checks a pocket.",
      pyromancer: "I smashed a limb and its blow flew off along the bark to its neighbour round his side, and the neighbour threw both! Two for one, cheeky. Whoever holds a graft passes the lot on. So I went for the heir next, loudly.",
      thermist: "A limb breaks and its move passes to the next limb round his side, which does its own and then the borrowed one. A limb already carrying hands on everything. Cold, tidy arithmetic. I counted who inherited before I swung again.",
      driftblade: "A limb broke and its move ran along to the next limb round his side, so that one did both, back to back, and a limb already carrying passed on the lot, so I swept the heir next, before its turn came round, and ran on.",
      alembist: "Observed: a broken limb's move is grafted onto the next limb round his side, which then performs both in order. A limb carrying grafts hands them all on. Prescription: break the heir before its double turn arrives.",
      threadweaver: "A cut limb's thread is tied on to the next limb round his side, which then works both patterns, one after the other. One already carrying hands on the whole bundle. Watch the heir; snip it before its turn comes double.",
      marionettist: "A fallen limb hands its part to the next limb round his side, which plays both scenes back to back. One already carrying hands on the lot. (Understudies, all of them.) I read the heir before calling the next break.",
      cleric: "A limb broke, and its blow was not gone. It passed to the next limb round his side, which did both, one after the other. One already carrying hands on all it held. I watched the heir, and kept everyone ready for two.",
      grovekeeper: "Prune one limb and its blow is grafted onto the next one round his side, which bears both. A limb already grafted hands on the lot. I looked to the heir before the next cut, since it would do two turns' work in one.",
      chronomancer: "The limb breaks, and a moment later its move is the next limb's too, the next one round his side, which does both in turn. A limb carrying hands on everything. I knew the heir before the break, and so did everyone.",
      tidecaller: "A limb broke and its blow was not lost; it ran down the bark to the next limb round his side, as water finds a new channel. That limb then did both. A limb carrying hands on everything. I watched the heir.",
      lanternkeeper: "When a limb broke, its blow was carried to the next limb round his side, like a lamp handed along. That limb did both. One already carrying handed on every one. I lit the heir so everyone watched it.",
      kenseier: "A broken limb leaves its stroke to the next limb round his side, which strikes both in turn, its own first. One already carrying hands on all it holds. I weighed the heir before choosing the next limb to break.",
      houndmaster: "Break a limb and its move goes to the next limb round his side, and that one does both, one after the other. Limb already carrying hands on the lot. Had a look at the heir before the dogs went in.",
    } },
  },
};
